\par\par \b \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's Pay Tops That Of Any Recent Tech Startup\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin, Nate Rattner and Bradley Olson \par \uc2 470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is paying employees more than any tech startup in recent history, according to financial data it has shown investors.\par \par \uc2 The company's stock-based compensation is about $1.5 million an employee, on average, across its workforce of roughly 4,000.\par \par \uc2 That is more than seven times higher than the stock-based pay \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 's \uc2 Google\uc2  disclosed in 2003, before it filed for an initial public offering in 2004. The $1.5 million is about 34 times the average employee compensation of 18 other large tech companies in the year before they went public, according to a Wall Street Journal analysis of data compiled by \uc2 Equilar\uc2 . The analysis reviewed major tech IPOs over the last 25 years.\par \par \uc2 The compensation figures have been adjusted into 2025 dollars to account for inflation.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  spokesman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 To keep its lead in the AI race, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is doling out massive stock compensation packages to top researchers and engineers, making them some of the richest employees in Silicon Valley. The equity awards are inflating the company's heavy operating losses and diluting existing shareholders at a rapid clip.\par \par \uc2 As an AI arms race intensified this summer, frontier labs such as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  faced pressure to increase employee pay after \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  began offering pay packages worth hundreds of millions of dollars -- and in some rare cases $1 billion -- to top executives and researchers at rivals.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg's recruiting blitz swept up 20-plus \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  personnel, including ChatGPT co-creator Shengjia Zhao. In August, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  gave some of its research and engineering staff a one-time bonus, with some employees receiving millions of dollars, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 The financial data, shared with investors over the summer, show that \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's stock-based compensation was expected to increase by about $3 billion annually through 2030.\par \par \uc2 The company recently told staff it would discontinue a policy that required employees to work at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  for at least six months before their equity vests. That development could lead to further compensation increases.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's compensation as a percentage of revenue was set to reach 46% in 2025, the highest of any of the 18 companies except for \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , which didn't generate revenue the year before its IPO. \uc2 Palantir\uc2 's stock-based compensation equaled 33% of its revenue the year before its initial public offering in 2020, \uc2 Google\uc2 's was 15% and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's was 6%, the analysis shows.\par \par \uc2 On average, each company's stock-based compensation made up about 6% of revenue among tech companies the Journal analyzed in the year before their IPO, according to the \uc2 Equilar\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , owner of the Journal, has a content-licensing partnership with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw394\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5910\pichgoal9600
