\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Time to Spend Your FSA Money Before Employers Get What's Left\b0\par\par\uc2 By Imani Moise \par \uc2 643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Americans leave more than $4 billion in flexible-spending accounts each year, money that goes straight back to their employers.\par \par \uc2 Medical FSAs let workers set aside up to $3,300 of pretax income this year to pay for out-of-pocket healthcare expenses such as copays, prescriptions and certain medical supplies. Unlike with health savings accounts, which allow unused balances to roll over indefinitely, money left in FSAs is generally forfeited at the end of the year. That often leads to a different kind of spending spree during the holidays.\par \par \uc2 About half of the 20 million Americans with \uc2 FSA\uc2  accounts let some of their funds expire each year. The average amount forfeited is over $400, according to the most recent data from the \uc2 Employee Benefit Research Institute\uc2 , or EBRI.\par \par \uc2 Is there any way to hold on to my money?\par \par \uc2 About two-thirds of employers now offer a grace period to use their FSA money, or let employees roll over a limited amount to the next year. Employers can allow a maximum rollover amount of $660 into next year. So it is worth checking your plan's rules before you rush to spend any remaining money on items you might not really need.\par \par \uc2 "Employers are broadly motivated to keep their employees happy, and generally people are not happy when they're forfeiting funds back to their employers," said Jake Spiegel, senior researcher at EBRI.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 IRS\uc2  rules, leftover money can be redistributed to employees. But the money has to be spread equally among all plan participants, not just the workers who forfeited funds.\par \par \uc2 In practice, employers typically end up using forfeited funds to reduce their overall administrative costs, said Chris Byrd, senior vice president at employee-benefits provider WEX.\par \par \uc2 What can you do with money that is about to expire?\par \par \uc2 Eligible expenses extend well beyond doctor visits. Online orders for menstrual products, pimple patches and first-aid supplies typically surge in the final month of the year, according to FSA Store, an online marketplace for FSA-eligible items.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, some products have become newly eligible, such as red-light therapy masks, \uc2 Oura\uc2  rings and lip balms (including the tinted variety). Higher cost and more unusual items usually require a doctor's note, but companies like FSA Store and Truemed have streamlined that process at check out.\par \par \uc2 Eligibility depends on an employer's specific plan, so confirm what qualifies before making last-minute purchases.\par \par \uc2 How do I strategize for the new year?\par \par \uc2 One underappreciated advantage of FSAs is that your full annual contribution is available on the first day of the plan year, even though the money is deducted incrementally from paychecks throughout the year. That effectively gives workers an interest-free advance on a chunk of their salary.\par \par \uc2 If you were to deplete your account and then leave your job before the year ends, you walk away having spent money that you never had to pay in. Employers also use forfeitures to cover those costs.\par \par \uc2 Even if you end up leaving FSA funds unspent, you could still come out ahead.\par \par \uc2 The money that goes into an FSA avoids federal income taxes, payroll taxes and, in many cases, state income taxes. So if a worker in the 22% tax bracket puts in $1,500, they could save as much as around $500 in taxes, said financial planner Allan Roth of Wealth Logic in Colorado Springs, Colo. That means the worker would have to forfeit more than $500 to wipe out their tax benefit.\par \par \uc2 Roth still recommends putting aside 10% less than you expect to spend. That helps prevent the annual year-end worries about leaving money on the table. Workers who enrolled in FSAs for 2026 were allowed to set aside as much as $3,400.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251224elco0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
