\par\par \b \uc2 Robotics Expert Joins Race For Top GM Job --- Co-founder of self-driving trucking company joined automaker in June\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep and Suzanne Vranica \par \uc2 1,323 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Sterling Anderson joined \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in 2014. A Business & Finance article on Monday about Anderson, who is the global product chief for \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , incorrectly said he joined \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in 2015.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Dec. 23, 2025)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 Sterling Anderson\uc2  clashed with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and left \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to launch a startup that has put self-driving trucks on the road. This summer he took a top job at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, the robotics guru is seen by senior GM leaders as a dark-horse candidate to succeed Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , who has led the automaker for more than a decade and turns 64 this week.\par \par \uc2 Anderson, 42 years old, joined GM in June as its global product chief, overseeing the development of all gasoline-powered and electric vehicles. He has expanded his influence in recent months, taking over units that develop artificial intelligence and integrate software into GM vehicles.\par \par \uc2 His rise is shaking up a succession landscape complicated by years of tumult and turnover in GM's headquarters. Anderson's decision to leave \uc2 Aurora Innovation\uc2 , the self-driving trucking company he co-founded and that is now valued at $8 billion, to join the ranks of GM left some puzzled.\par \par \uc2 "Sterling saw GM as kind of a company that has lost its way a little bit and saw an opportunity to go and help," said \uc2 Aurora\uc2  co-founder and CEO \uc2 Chris Urmson\uc2 . "He thought he could go there and help point the right direction."\par \par \uc2 Barra has run GM since 2014 -- a tenure that is more than double the median S&P 500 CEO's term. She introduced EV models and steered the company through the Trump administration's tariffs. Asked recently during an onstage interview about how long she plans to remain at GM, Barra said, "That's the board's decision, not mine."\par \par \uc2 A GM spokeswoman said there has been no discussion of Barra leaving. As for Anderson, she said, "Any discussion of a future role is premature and speculative."\par \par \uc2 Several other GM executives are also seen by investors as candidates for the top job. They include finance chief \uc2 Paul Jacobson\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Reuss\uc2 , a 40-year GM veteran and Barra's No. 2.\par \par \uc2 Other would-be CEO candidates have left the company in recent years. Among them: \uc2 Dhivya Suryadevara\uc2 , a high-profile finance chief in 2020, \uc2 Dan Ammann\uc2 , Barra's former No. 2 in 2021, and Marissa West, a rising star who left last year just eight months after joining GM's senior leadership team.\par \par \uc2 In choosing its next leader, the automaker's board faces a fundamental question: Is GM a tech company capable of competing with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Waymo\uc2 , or an old-school vehicle manufacturer married to large gas-guzzling vehicles?\par \par \uc2 Barra prefers the former, and Anderson is the latest in a string of Silicon Valley transplants she has hired at GM. The newcomer is one of a small group of executives who sit on GM's influential executive strategy team. Anderson, however, is a relative unknown on Wall Street, especially compared with Jacobson, who is a former \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  CFO, and Reuss, who was a contender for the CEO title when it went to Barra.\par \par \uc2 The Silicon Valley aura has proved elusive to GM, as it has to other automakers chasing the same strategy. The company ditched plans last year for its Cruise robotaxi program after spending $10 billion and a decade on its development. It has suffered setbacks around its EV and autonomous-driving strategies. The company in October booked a $1.6 billion charge on its EV business as demand for the vehicles sinks.\par \par \uc2 A parade of executives plucked from \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  have come and gone in quick succession, while GM lags behind \uc2 Waymo\uc2  and Chinese players such as \uc2 Baidu\uc2  and \uc2 WeRide\uc2  in developing a system that allows drivers to take their eyes off the road.\par \par \uc2 "The way things are going, you just want someone from Silicon Valley to stick around for more than six to nine months," said \uc2 Morningstar\uc2  analyst \uc2 David Whiston\uc2 , who has covered the automotive industry since 2007.\par \par \uc2 Since Anderson joined, a trio of high-ranking tech executives have left GM. One of them, the company's software-engineering chief, left a week after appearing on stage alongside Anderson and Barra to roll out a new tech strategy.\par \par \uc2 "Our strategy is bigger than any one leader, and it's working," the GM spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 The new tech strategy, helmed by Anderson, aims to bring eyes-off driving to the market in 2028 in an electric Cadillac SUV. It also includes making a lower-cost battery and introducing artificial-intelligence into GM vehicles like the Chevy Silverado.\par \par \uc2 A soft-spoken technophile with a doctorate in robotics from the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 , Anderson has a collection of patents and papers on autonomous technology. Asked recently to outline GM's tech strategy, he described a vision of future electric vehicles with "much higher computational ceiling or headroom."\par \par \uc2 He went on to clarify: "What effectively that means for you practically in your life is that your car will get better the longer you own it."\par \par \uc2 Within GM, Anderson's arrival was cheered by many employees involved in the automaker's self-driving technology, still stinging from GM's move last year to end the Cruise program. Anderson has rehired some key Cruise staffers and, in internal meetings, praised the unit and its progress on the technology.\par \par \uc2 Cruise was an early mover in the robotaxi race. But its reputation suffered from alarming mishaps and an accident in California that prompted GM to pull the cars from several markets. The automaker said it scrapped the program because of rising competition and costs.\par \par \uc2 Employees in wider parts of the GM universe are less sold on Anderson. Some question the wisdom of bringing back Cruise leaders, given missteps at the unit. The new boss irked some within GM's ranks when, in a recent town hall, he defended the company's use of a performance-ranking system to grade employees.\par \par \uc2 Anderson didn't respond to requests for comment and GM declined to make him available. In a statement provided by GM, Anderson said he was concerned that he might be perceived as a "Silicon Valley cowboy pew-pewing his way" through GM.\par \par \uc2 "While some tension is inevitable through transformations, the team understands, appreciates, and is already making great strides as it accelerates and broadens the pace of innovation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Anderson codes in his free time and plays in a Silicon Valley hockey league. Where Barra has spent her entire career at GM, starting there in college more than 40 years ago, Anderson has moved around, working at McKinsey before joining \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in 2015.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Anderson led development of the Model X SUV and the company's Autopilot system. He butted heads with Musk as the two worked to develop cars that drive themselves.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, he resigned from \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in part over the company's marketing of a new self-driving feature. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  sued Anderson, accusing him of taking proprietary documents and improperly colluding to recruit Autopilot employees to work at \uc2 Aurora\uc2 . Anderson said the lawsuit was meritless; \uc2 Aurora\uc2  eventually agreed to pay \uc2 Tesla\uc2  $100,000 as part of a settlement.\par \par \uc2 Anderson went on to spend nearly a decade at \uc2 Aurora\uc2  and led development of a Lidar system that bounces lasers off objects to capture surroundings in 3-D.\par \par \uc2 Aurora\uc2  has self-driving trucks on the road in Texas. The vehicles are delivering freight between Fort Worth and El Paso and Dallas and Houston, as well as hauling fracking sand on public roads and highways in the Permian Basin.\par \par \uc2 The company has hit most of its milestones, but it has also faced setbacks. In spring 2025, two weeks after putting trucks on the road without drivers, \uc2 Aurora\uc2  had to add human observers at the request of one of the manufacturers it partners with. \uc2 Aurora\uc2 's share price has languished amid questions of how readily it can amass customers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251222elcm0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
