\par\par \b \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Wins Fight Over Musk Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Becky Peterson and Theo Francis \par \uc2 910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  won its fight over a record-breaking pay package for Chief Executive \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that was approved by shareholders in 2018, ending the yearslong legal battle over his compensation that grew in value to total $139 billion.\par \par \uc2 In an opinion issued Friday, the Delaware Supreme Court said the lower court's ruling would have deprived Musk of compensation for several years of work, and therefore he should get the full pay package.\par \par \uc2 The court's opinion reversed the 2024 decision from Delaware's Chancery Court to rescind Musk's pay package after finding that \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's directors were beholden to its CEO and that the approval process for the pay deal was tainted and lacked transparency.\par \par \uc2 "Because it is the unmaking of an agreement, rescission is an extreme remedy and should only be granted by a court of equity when it is 'clearly warranted,'" the court wrote in the unsigned opinion.\par \par \uc2 Though Musk's pay package was reinstated, the five judges said they had "varying views" on \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's liability, and the ruling ultimately affirmed part of the lower-court decision. They awarded $1 in nominal damages to Richard Tornetta, a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholder who argued that Musk was unjustly awarded what was at the time the largest pay package in history.\par \par \uc2 The court also found that Tornetta's attorneys were entitled to some fees. The lower court had previously awarded them $345 million. In a statement, lawyers for the plaintiff said the case called "to account the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  board and its largest stockholder for their breaches of fiduciary duty."\par \par \uc2 The decision doesn't affect the more recent pay package that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders approved for Musk in November, which could earn the chief executive as much as $1 trillion over a 10-year period if the company meets a set of ambitious targets.\par \par \uc2 That package was issued under Texas law, where \uc2 Tesla\uc2  redomiciled last year, and which corporate attorneys say is likely to prove less hospitable to lawsuits like the one over Musk's 2018 pay.\par \par \uc2 To earn the full value of the 2025 package, Musk must increase \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's market capitalization to $8.5 trillion, which is more than five times its current value. If achieved, Musk would surpass the 25% voting control threshold that he has said he wants to stay at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and build its artificial-intelligence business instead of focusing on his other companies.\par \par \uc2 But Friday's ruling makes it likely he will give up a second, $23.7 billion award known as the 2025 interim plan, which was designed to compensate him for some of the disputed shares but doesn't allow him to "double dip."\par \par \uc2 Musk's 2018 stock options have grown in value to $139 billion, which is more than the combined compensation realized by the CEOs of all other S&P 500 companies over the decade ended last year, according to \uc2 Equilar\uc2 , a pay data firm. At that valuation, the 2018 pay package is currently around 9% of the company's market capitalization.\par \par \uc2 Musk, the world's richest person, had worked at \uc2 Tesla\uc2  from 2018 to 2025 without pay beyond the disputed equity grant, though his personal wealth skyrocketed as \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's market cap surged to $1.6 trillion, where it now hovers.\par \par \uc2 Musk's 2018 pay package from \uc2 Tesla\uc2  was initially valued at more than $2.3 billion, shattering previous compensation records. It was contingent on \uc2 Tesla\uc2  meeting a series of market-value and operating targets. Meeting those targets delivered the underlying stock options to him, while dramatic share-price appreciation swelled their value many times over.\par \par \uc2 The court's decision brings an end to the legal battle brought on by Tornetta, the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholder who sued in 2018, arguing that \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's board had failed to meet Delaware's tough requirements for disclosure and fairness when dealing with a dominant shareholder.\par \par \uc2 In January 2024, Delaware Chancellor Kathaleen McCormick ruled in Tornetta's favor, finding the company hadn't been sufficiently transparent with shareholders and that Musk had exerted too much influence over the process that led to his pay package.\par \par \uc2 In the nearly-two years since that ruling, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's board has aggressively campaigned for Musk's compensation plans both in and out of the courtroom, arguing that an ambitious package is necessary to keep his attention focused on the electric vehicle maker as it changes strategy to focus on robotics and artificial intelligence\par \par \uc2 Last year, the board tried to reinstate the 2018 package through a shareholder vote at the June 2024 annual meeting. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders approved the pay package for a second time with an overwhelming 72% majority, but the vote was largely symbolic because it didn't alter the court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, shareholders approved the board's request to redomicile in the state of Texas, where it is headquartered, bringing an end to its legal relationship with Delaware. Musk and other \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholders have since become vocal advocates for other corporations leaving the East Coast state, which is popular with businesses due to its long history of corporate litigation.\par \par \uc2 In March, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and board members appealed the lower court decision, arguing that stockholders twice approved the pay deal and that by attempting to nullify it, it would be unfair to investors. The board also contended that the lower court was flawed in concluding that Musk could coerce or dominate directors during compensation negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251220elck0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
