\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Railroad Operators Seek U.S. Regulator's Approval For Coast-to-Coast Merger\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kelly Cloonan \par \uc2 315 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  and \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  filed an application with the \uc2 Surface Transportation Board\uc2  requesting approval of their proposed merger.\par \par \uc2 The application, filed Friday, said the merger would boost competition, streamlining pricing of interline moves for thousands of customer locations. It also would allow the industry to compete more effectively with long-haul trucking, the companies said.\par \par \uc2 The deal also would move freight more efficiently and preserve all union jobs that exist at the time of the merger, according to the application. The growth of the combined company also is expected to create about 900 net new union jobs by the third year following the merger.\par \par \uc2 The application includes 2,000 letters of support from stakeholders, the companies said.\par \par \uc2 The companies' proposed $71.5 billion merger, announced in July, would form a single company controlling coast-to-coast rail shipments for the first time in U.S. history. Regulators have been skeptical of deals that could create a transcontinental rail juggernaut, worrying that they could result in price increases, service disruptions and lower investment in safety improvements.\par \par \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2 , \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 's direct competitor, said it remains opposed to the proposed merger after seeing the application. The company argued the deal poses long-term threats to competition, leaving shippers with fewer options and driving up rates and ultimately higher prices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 "This didn't begin with customers asking for this merger, and the claimed public benefits appear to accrue primarily to shareholders," \uc2 BNSF\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Katie Farmer\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Pacific Kansas City similarly voiced concerns over the merger, saying in a Friday statement that it would pose risks to customers, rail employees and broader supply chains. "The proposed UP-NS merger, unprecedented in scale and scope, would radically and permanently change the U.S. rail network," it added.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251220elck0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
