\par\par \b \uc2 Air-Safety Worker Bonuses Backfired\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal and Allison Pohle \par \uc2 788 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bonus payments aim to motivate hard workers and inspire them to go the extra mile. For many air-traffic controllers and airport security officers, they are having the opposite effect.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown this fall, the Trump administration pledged $10,000 bonuses to hundreds of air-traffic controllers, technicians and \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  officers, intended to recognize workers who consistently came in despite working without pay.\par \par \uc2 But only a small number of controllers qualified for the bonuses. Federal agencies said the awards were coming to 311 unionized controllers out of a workforce of about 13,000, 423 unionized technicians out of at least 6,000 and an unspecified number of TSA officers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , which represents TSA officers, has sought records detailing the criteria for the bonuses, saying the eligibility requirements weren't clear. The \uc2 National Air Traffic Controllers Association\uc2  labor union has said it is concerned about the exclusion of thousands of controllers who consistently reported for duty during the shutdown with minimal time off.\par \par \uc2 "Recognition should bring the workforce together, not divide it," wrote Nick Daniels, the air-traffic controller union's president, last month in an internal memo reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 The Transportation Department said that the Trump administration has sought to pay controllers quickly after the shutdown and that individuals who went above and beyond were rewarded. The agency said efforts are under way to expand staffing and upgrade technology.\par \par \uc2 Debate over bonus payments for aviation safety workers is part of the broader fallout from the 43-day government shutdown, with federal officials also probing workers' attendance and airlines' compliance with mandated flight restrictions. The federal government is currently funded through the end of January, raising the prospect of another shutdown in the near future.\par \par \uc2 Abby Inglee, a transportation security officer at \uc2 Tampa International Airport\uc2 , was one of 122 officers at the airport to receive a $10,000 bonus. Inglee said she had scheduled a couple of days off for day trips during the government shutdown, but canceled them to save money.\par \par \uc2 The 26-year-old said she learned she would receive a bonus Dec. 8. "It makes me happy that I did cancel my leave," said Inglee, who has worked for the agency for four years. "It gives us a boost of morale internally, and outside it shows recognition to the public."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  has announced bonuses airport by airport, including for officers at airports in Houston, Boston and Tampa, Fla.\par \par \uc2 A TSA spokesman said that bonuses were awarded to officers who "went above and beyond their typical duties by delivering excellent service and keeping America's skies safe."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  for years has struggled to fully staff control towers and facilities, often resulting in air-traffic controllers working six-day workweeks and 10-hour days.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has said it is working to boost staffing, streamlining its hiring process and offering cash incentives to recruits.\par \par \uc2 The agency said it has brought in more than 2,000 new controllers in its 2025 fiscal year, ahead of its schedule.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said in a November social-media post that he would recommend that air-traffic controllers who didn't take off time during the shutdown should each receive a $10,000 bonus. He also threatened to dock the pay of controllers who didn't return to work.\par \par \uc2 Some air-traffic controllers had called out of work as the shutdown ground along, leading the \uc2 FAA\uc2  to restrict flights. Union officials said that some controllers had grown financially strained, and worked other jobs to help pay for child care and household staples.\par \par \uc2 The bonus payments' perfect attendance requirement for air-traffic controllers, which included overtime shifts, has amplified frustrations, according to controllers. Awards have been denied for controllers who missed as much as an hour of work due to fatigue, military leave or time off that was scheduled as much as a year in advance, they said.\par \par \uc2 "They are disappointed, as there were thousands of [air-traffic controllers] that kept the system safe and running during the shutdown," the controllers' union said.\par \par \uc2 When asked in late November about potential awards for controllers who missed a day or two of work during the shutdown, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2  said that he and \uc2 FAA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Bryan Bedford\uc2  had publicly recognized difficult circumstances faced by all controllers during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 "We have to look and say 'where's the cutoff,' " Duffy said at a press event at Newark Liberty International Airport. "If you got a 100% on your test, you get the sticker -- that's a scratch-and-sniff sticker from the administration."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251218elci00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
