\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobs in Focus With Arrival of Delayed Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng \par \uc2 478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For months, a crucial question has been hanging over the economy: What is really going on in the labor market?\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the country will finally start to get some answers.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department, after pausing its data collection for weeks during the government shutdown, will publish a report with two months of data on the health of the U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 The report will reveal the unemployment rate for November and some data on hiring in October. That information looks backward, but is expected to fill in the blanks on the status of the job market.\par \par \uc2 The report won't include the unemployment rate for October. The Labor Department couldn't conduct the survey needed to calculate that rate while the government was shut down. It will mark the first time in nearly 80 years that the agency is unable to calculate the jobless rate.\par \par \uc2 Recently, the U.S. job market has been in an uneasy balance. Hiring has fallen beneath its pre-Covid level, a consequence of cooling demand for workers but also slower growth in the number of people seeking jobs due to government restrictions on immigrants. Changing tariff policies and increased costs are among the reasons why companies don't want to load up on new workers. Hopes by company executives that artificial intelligence can handle more work has even led to some white-collar layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The last time there was a regularly scheduled jobs report was the August report, released Sept. 5. The most recent available report, for September, was only published last month. It showed stronger-than-expected hiring with 119,000 jobs added, although the unemployment rate rose to 4.4%, the highest in four years.\par \par \uc2 Since then, economists have leaned on private-sector indicators to gauge the labor market, including reports from the human-resources firm ADP. Those signals point to patchy hiring and a labor market that continues to cool but not collapse.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. likely lost jobs in October due to tens of thousands of workers coming off federal payrolls, the result of a deferred-resignation program launched earlier in the year. Economists say that as that effect wears off, job growth in November should turn positive but remain subdued.\par \par \uc2 "Businesses are still profitable and growing on the whole, so there's not really a catalyst for laying off people," said Stephen Juneau, a U.S. economist for \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 . But "we're still in a low-fire, no-hire environment."\par \par \uc2 The release on Tuesday will likely include the usual revisions for previous months. Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said last week that official statistics could be overestimating job creation by up to 60,000 jobs a month, meaning the U.S. could have lost 20,000 jobs a month since April.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251216elcg0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
