\par\par \b \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  Shortens Vesting Period --- Maker of ChatGPT relaxes curbs meant to keep new hires from leaving firm\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky, Berber Jin and Georgia Wells \par \uc2 629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  told staff this past week that it was ending a compensation policy that required employees to work at the company for at least six months before their equity vests.\par \par \uc2 The change to the "vesting cliff," announced by applications chief Fidji Simo, is designed to encourage new employees to take risks without fear of being let go before accessing their first chunk of equity, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  shortened its vesting period for new employees to six months from the industry standard of 12 months in April. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 xAI\uc2 , an \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  competitor, made a similar change in the late summer, people familiar with the change said.\par \par \uc2 XAI\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The decision to loosen or do away with restrictions meant to ensure new hires stick around reflects the frenzied competition for top-tier technical talent within the AI industry. Tech companies typically have a one-year vesting cliff for new employees, preventing them from having to give away stock to hires who leave quickly or don't work out.\par \par \uc2 But with AI companies including \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  wooing top researchers with pay packages that can be worth $100 million or more, researchers and engineers have been able to hold out for the most-attractive terms and in many cases have been quick to leave jobs they have found not to their liking.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is already doling out far more stock-based compensation than other tech companies, owing to an intense talent war it is waging with its competitors. The company expects to spend $6 billion this year on such costs -- almost half of its projected revenue -- according to financial documents sent to investors over the summer and viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Tech investors have privately complained about the ballooning stock-based compensation associated with fast-growing AI startups, arguing that it eats into shareholder returns.\par \par \uc2 "Companies that are needing to be more competitive are dropping the traditional first-year vesting cliff," said Zaheer Mohiuddin, co-founder of Levels.fyi, a platform that gathers data on compensation in tech careers.\par \par \uc2 In August, after \uc2 Meta\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  launched a full-scale raid of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's staff and offered giant pay packages, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  gave some of its top researchers and engineers a one-time bonus, with some employees receiving millions of dollars, the Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 xAI\uc2  earlier this year, hiring managers were having to work harder to persuade potential recruits to be interviewed, according to people involved in hiring decisions at the time. Already facing a rash of departures and reputation issues, \uc2 xAI\uc2  quietly made the decision in the third quarter to shorten the vesting period for new hires, according to people familiar with the change.\par \par \uc2 Musk, who has said he works "every waking hour," is known for expecting long, grueling hours of his employees and burning through executives who struggle to keep up with his demands. Since the summer, \uc2 xAI\uc2  has lost leaders in charge of X, legal, finance and engineering. One legal executive announced his departure on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  with a meme of a man in a suit shoveling coal.\par \par \uc2 In late 2024 and early 2025, Musk's close political alliance with Donald Trump and his outspokenness on right-wing culture-war issues increasingly posed a challenge for his companies, with many \uc2 Tesla\uc2  buyers souring on the brand and at least two engineers publicly saying they left jobs at the company over Musk's political activities.\par \par \uc2 The rate at which recruits have been accepting \uc2 xAI\uc2 's offers has increased since the startup implemented the shorter period, they said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251215elcf0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
