\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Tells Staff It Is Getting Ready For Potential IPO\b0\par\par\uc2 By Micah Maidenberg \par \uc2 531 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  told employees it is preparing for a possible public offering next year, confirming a potential listing for a company that has built formidable rocket and satellite businesses since \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  founded it in 2002.\par \par \uc2 "The thinking is that if we execute brilliantly and the markets cooperate, a public offering could raise a significant amount of capital," \uc2 Bret Johnsen\uc2 , the company's finance chief, wrote Friday in a message to \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  staff.\par \par \uc2 Johnsen said that whether a listing would happen and when it might take place were "highly uncertain," according to the message, a copy of which was reviewed by The Wall Street Journal. He didn't discuss a valuation the company could target in an IPO.\par \par \uc2 Johnsen disclosed in his memo a new stock price of $421 a share, which would value \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  at around $800 billion, people familiar with the company said. That would be up from a valuation of $400 billion earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  didn't respond to requests for comment. The Journal and other news outlets have reported the Texas-based company was weighing a public offering in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Potential IPO proceeds could be used to ramp up launches of the company's Starship rocket to "an insane flight rate," deploy artificial-intelligence data centers in space, and work on uncrewed and human missions to Mars, Johnsen wrote.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is the world's busiest rocket launcher, frequently using its fleet of Falcon 9 vehicles to take customer satellites and its own Starlink satellites to low-Earth-orbit. Starlink, which uses more than 9,000 satellites to provide high-speed internet connections, has grown quickly in recent years.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's decision to explore going public has surprised some people close to the company, which has long been dedicated to pursuing Musk's dream of sending humans to Mars.\par \par \uc2 A dozen years ago, Musk said taking \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  public would only be possible when ships were regularly flying to Mars. "Mars requires developing complex technology over a decade+, but market cares about next three months. Result would be conflicting priorities," he said in the 2013 social-media post.\par \par \uc2 Musk has voiced frustration with running \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in the public markets, sparring with securities regulators and battling investor lawsuits over his compensation at the electric-vehicle maker.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  also has a well-honed method for providing employees and former staffers with opportunities to periodically cash out their shares in the privately held company. It typically offers employees and former staffers two opportunities each year to sell some of their shareholdings.\par \par \uc2 "I think the reason to take any company public is to give employees liquidity, and we have been able to give employees liquidity," \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  President \uc2 Gwynne Shotwell\uc2  told reporters in 2023 when asked about a potential spinoff of the company's Starlink division.\par \par \uc2 As a privately held company, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  doesn't report its financials, but Musk said earlier this year that he expected it to generate around $15.5 billion in revenue this year. This month, he described \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  as "cash flow positive for many years" in a post on X, the social-media platform he owns.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251213elcd0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
