\par\par \b \uc2 Nissan\uc2  Lost Way, Says Carmaker's U.S. Boss\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Otts \par \uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN, Tenn. -- When \uc2 Christian Meunier\uc2  took over \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's Americas business at the start of the year, he said the company was lost.\par \par \uc2 "There was no North Star. There was no vision. There was no direction," Meunier said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The automaker had been slow to make decisions and was complacent with its sinking status in the U.S., he said. One of Meunier's first actions was to order corporate employees at \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's Tennessee headquarters back to the office four days a week. Collaboration is "very difficult" from home and problems often go unsolved, he said.\par \par \uc2 But it will take a lot more than that to reverse \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's slide that began nearly a decade ago. \uc2 Nissan\uc2  reported its fifth-straight quarterly net loss last month and it is closing seven of its 17 factories globally and cutting 20,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The automaker has struggled to find direction since the arrest of its longtime chief executive, \uc2 Carlos Ghosn\uc2 , in 2018. Its plight -- as Japan's No. 3 carmaker -- became so dire that the Japanese government last year encouraged a rushed merger with its stronger rival \uc2 Honda\uc2 , but the tie-up fell apart.\par \par \uc2 Meunier's task is turning around the company in its biggest market, the U.S. Sales have fallen about 40% over the past decade, and its share of the market has dwindled to 6.4%, from a peak of 8.4% in 2017, according to \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2  dealers often struggle with higher inventory levels than their competitors and frequently have to discount products to get them off the lot and earn volume bonuses from the automaker.\par \par \uc2 "It isn't that their products aren't valuable, but you kind of train people that this is the cheap one," said Stephanie Brinley, North America auto analyst at S&P Global Mobility. "Being the cheapest available and the best deal isn't always the way to generate as much loyalty as you want."\par \par \uc2 Brinley said \uc2 Nissan\uc2  squandered its lead in the U.S. electric-vehicle market with the Leaf hatchback and waited too long to update its Frontier pickup truck despite the popularity of midsize trucks. "They've got a lot of repair to do," Brinley said.\par \par \uc2 Meunier, 58 years old, who worked at \uc2 Nissan\uc2  for 17 years on three continents before leaving in 2019 to run \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's Jeep brand, said his task is to rekindle the "fighting spirit" that led him to join the automaker in 2002. That means reversing a litany of mistakes from the past decade, from killing popular models such as the Xterra SUV to chronic underutilization at its two U.S. factories in Tennessee and Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2  also hasn't brought its gas-electric hybrid technology to the U.S., leaving the company without a hybrid at a time when the technology is taking off. At \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's larger Japanese rival \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , about half of its U.S. sales are hybrids.\par \par \uc2 Meunier is pulling \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's hybrid plans ahead, starting next year with its most-popular U.S. vehicle, the Rogue crossover. Eventually, he wants to have hybrids across \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's U.S. lineup.\par \par \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's U.S. plants are currently producing 600,000 vehicles annually but have capacity for one million, so Meunier wants to increase production. He also plans to bring back the Xterra, a rugged off-road vehicle that represents "the perfect combination of what \uc2 Nissan\uc2  is about -- the affordability, the fun and the durability," he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, he said he needs to improve relations with \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's dealers, some of whom have struggled to turn a profit and say they have been starved for fresh vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Alan Haig\uc2 , president of dealership brokerage firm \uc2 Haig Partners\uc2 , said \uc2 Nissan\uc2  has relied on offering incentives to dealers to meet sales goals to a greater extent than its competitors.\par \par \uc2 "The true level of demand was a good bit lower than what they were trying to sell," Haig said. Meunier said \uc2 Nissan\uc2  is still pushing its dealers with sales incentives because increasing sales is crucial for the brand's turnaround.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that things are improving, Meunier said. \uc2 Nissan\uc2 's share of U.S. retail sales was 4.9% in the most-recent quarter, up from 4.1% a year earlier. "You don't do that overnight, but the momentum is there," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
