\par\par \b \uc2 Court to Eye Vast Firing Power for President --- Justices are poised to strip agencies' broad job protections in an ex-\uc2 FTC\uc2  official's case\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Romoser \par \uc2 1,079 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Trump's firing spree at federal agencies has snowballed into a Supreme Court showdown over how modern American government should work.\par \par \uc2 Dangling by a thread is the tradition that certain sensitive policy details -- from regulating nuclear reactors to setting safety standards for consumer products -- should be managed by bipartisan panels of technocrats, whose jobs are insulated from political pressure.\par \par \uc2 In its place, the high court appears poised to enshrine a competing view championed by Trump: that presidents must have the power to dismiss any of these unelected experts for any reason -- or no reason at all. That is the only way, proponents say, to cut red tape and ensure that regulators remain accountable to voters.\par \par \uc2 "This case is at the heart of two conflicting visions for the presidency and how the government operates generally," said Ilan Wurman, an expert on administrative and constitutional law at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 . "Given what we know of the justices' prior views and decisions, it would be surprising if they didn't rule in favor of robust presidential control of the administrative state."\par \par \uc2 The court will hear arguments Monday in the case of \uc2 Rebecca Slaughter\uc2 , a Democratic member of the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  fired by Trump in March. The justices are considering what might seem like a technical issue involving the constitutionality of longstanding federal laws that restrict the president's ability to fire the leaders of independent agencies, including the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  and the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the court's answer, expected by July, will matter far beyond the job security of bureaucrats at boards and commissions with an alphabet soup of initials.\par \par \uc2 A broad victory for Trump will allow him to intensify his mission of directing those agencies to implement his deregulatory agenda, advance his political aims and even pursue retribution against his foes. It is all part of what conservative legal scholars call the unitary executive theory, the notion that the Constitution concentrates all the powers of the executive branch in the hands of a muscular president.\par \par \uc2 In an interview from her home, where she now works on her case, Slaughter said granting the president such unfettered power could have broad ramifications for the nation's economy.\par \par \uc2 "Congress set up these agencies to protect integrity and stability in markets, and to ensure that decisions -- many of which involve economic policy -- are being made by experts on the merits, rather than for political reasons or to do favors for the president's allies or punish his enemies," she said.\par \par \uc2 Trump began his firing spree just days after he returned to the White House. He targeted Democrats serving on more than a dozen federal panels, from regulatory behemoths like the \uc2 FTC\uc2  and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to more obscure bodies like the \uc2 Nuclear Regulatory Commission\uc2 , the \uc2 Surface Transportation Board\uc2 , and the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board.\par \par \uc2 The firings contradicted federal statutes that say the president can remove these officials only if they neglect their duties or commit other misconduct, a structure set up by Congress to give agencies a measure of protection from political meddling. The ouster of Slaughter also flouted a 1935 Supreme Court decision, known as Humphrey's Executor, that upheld the restriction on the president's ability to remove members of the \uc2 FTC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the Trump administration argues that all of these firing restrictions are unconstitutional, and it wants the court to overturn its 90-year-old ruling that fortified them.\par \par \uc2 "The notion that some agencies that exercise executive power can be sequestered from presidential control seriously offends the Constitution's structure and the liberties that the separation of powers protects," Trump's solicitor general, D. John Sauer, wrote in a brief to the court. The agencies at stake in the case "regulate innumerable aspects of modern life," and the officials who enact those regulations wield authority that ultimately belongs to the president, he said.\par \par \uc2 So far, the court's conservative majority has been receptive to the administration's position. In a series of emergency rulings this year, the justices allowed several high-profile dismissals, including Slaughter's, to take effect even though lower courts had blocked them.\par \par \uc2 Those emergency rulings were only temporary, but many legal experts see them as foreshadowing a permanent decision in the Slaughter case that broadly expands the president's power to reshape government agencies as he wishes.\par \par \uc2 Still, the justices may be wary of a sweeping decision that covers all regulatory bodies, particularly the \uc2 Federal Reserve's Board of Governors\uc2 . The court has already indicated -- in a cryptic unsigned passage in an emergency ruling in May -- that the Fed may be entitled to more independence than other federal boards.\par \par \uc2 Trump has tried to fire Fed board member Lisa Cook -- a dismissal the high court has kept on hold while it waits to hear Cook's case in January. Unlike with Slaughter and the other fired officials, Trump argues that he does have good "cause" under the relevant statute to oust Cook.\par \par \uc2 Industry stakeholders, consumer advocates and some of Trump's foes are already feeling the effects of the president's bid to consolidate control over the administrative state.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Corporation for Public Broadcasting\uc2 , the independent body that funds \uc2 PBS\uc2  and \uc2 NPR\uc2 , Trump's dismissal of three board members this spring was one of his first steps in his campaign against public media.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2 , the agency in April proposed a major safety regulation for lithium-ion batteries often found in e-bikes and scooters. The commission's three Democrats supported the regulation, while its two Republicans opposed it. But after Trump fired all three Democrats, the Republican members withdrew the proposal.\par \par \uc2 And at the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , after Trump fired Slaughter and another Democrat, the agency launched an investigation into the liberal nonprofit Media Matters, and began probing allegedly false claims by medical providers who help transgender patients transition.\par \par \uc2 Armed with unlimited authority to fire top regulators, it is hard to see why any future president would tolerate the presence of any agency official from the opposite political party who might dissent, legal experts say.\par \par \uc2 "The ability to remove becomes the ability to control the whole agenda," said \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  law professor Victoria Nourse, "and to undermine Congress's power to create the office that it wants."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251208elc80001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
