\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Private Hiring Fell Last Month, ADP Estimates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman and Chao Deng \par \uc2 222 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hiring by American businesses dropped last month, according to an estimate from payroll processor ADP, a further setback for a labor market that has slowed this year.\par \par \uc2 Private employers shed 32,000 jobs in November, ADP estimated, a swing from the 47,000 private-sector jobs that ADP estimated were added in October. Analysts surveyed by The Wall Street Journal were expecting to see 40,000 new private-sector jobs.\par \par \uc2 ADP's numbers, which come from the anonymized payrolls of ADP's corporate clients, have long been on economists' radar. Since October, though, they have taken on special importance given the vacuum of official economic data caused by the recent government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , still in catch-up mode three weeks after the shutdown ended, won't release its look at the November jobs picture until Dec. 16, 2<frac12> weeks behind schedule.\par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 BLS\uc2  report arrives later this month, it will present a more comprehensive picture of the job market that also includes the public sector, with data on how the government shutdown and job losses for federal workers shaped overall employment. The government figures are based on an official survey that may reach a broader, more diverse set of companies.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
