\par\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Delivery Businesses Seek to \uc2 Unite\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean McLain \par \uc2 776 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  is facing a new labor challenge, this time from small-business owners who run the company's package-delivery network.\par \par \uc2 A group calling itself DSPs for Equitable and Fair Treatment, or Deft, went public on Black Friday, saying its goal is to organize the company's roughly 2,400 delivery-service providers to fight for better terms and reverse policies they say wipe out their profits.\par \par \uc2 The founders, who have yet to reveal their identities in public, are aiming for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to increase pay for package deliveries and reimbursement for van usage, and loosen the criteria for bonus payouts.\par \par \uc2 The group has been in the works since this summer, when delivery-fleet owners started receiving surprise bills for van repairs, some surpassing $100,000. An outcry by owners on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's internal forums, partly organized in encrypted Signal chats, led to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  reversing the fees. The victory inspired Deft's founders to create a formal organization as a chapter of the American Association of Franchisees and Dealers.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has faced attempts to organize warehouse workers and truck drivers. The company has opposed those efforts but also raised wages for the groups.\par \par \uc2 Deft's founders are hoping to obtain similar concessions by signing up enough delivery companies to force \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to give them a say in new policies, or reverse old ones.\par \par \uc2 "My hope is that DEFT will be a voice for the voiceless. DEFT is the answer to those of you who are pleading to help and feel you're screaming into the void," one of the group's founders wrote in a Signal chat with fellow owners viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "The vast majority of delivery service partners in our program are running successful, profitable businesses, and claims to the contrary are factually inaccurate," an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson said. "Our data shows DSP profitability is strong and has consistently improved year over year."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson said the company consults with a group of DSPs about policy changes.\par \par \uc2 Previous attempts to unionize other areas of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's business have been met with resistance, with some workers alleging they were fired in retaliation for wanting to join a union. Last December, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees at seven warehouses joined the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  to go on strike in part because the company refused to recognize the union's representation.\par \par \uc2 In building Deft's membership, the founders are taking steps to shield members' identities and communications from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The group is in the initial stages of vetting applicants to ensure they won't relay information to the company, according to a post in the DSP Signal chat. After consulting with a military veteran, the founders decided to create a structure of five-person "cells" to keep members of the larger organization anonymous in the event one cell is compromised. It remains uncertain whether the organization will gain enough momentum to effectively negotiate with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Deft's public debut during the busiest shopping season highlights the challenges business owners say their companies face. Delivery firms have been ramping up hiring and renting spare vans to keep pace with the holiday rush, but many DSPs say they lose money during the season despite the increased revenue because their costs also balloon.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  reimburses owners for vans based on the number of routes they are supposed to run, but many company owners say they must keep a higher number of vehicles on hand than \uc2 Amazon\uc2  budgets for to account for breakdowns or other issues. Those extra vans eat into a delivery company's earnings, but the alternative of dropping a route can lead to lost bonuses and reduced delivery loads.\par \par \uc2 Fleet owners say they have been contending with rising insurance costs and other operating expenses that have eroded margins since the DSP program started, while \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's fee schedule has stayed largely flat.\par \par \uc2 In September, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it would increase per-package payments to DSPs by 2 cents and increase the minimum wage for delivery drivers. Deft's founders are aiming for larger fee increases that keep pace with their rising costs.\par \par \uc2 One of the group's main targets is a new scoring system \uc2 Amazon\uc2  uses to rate the performance of delivery companies. In addition to being rated for on-time delivery and driver safety, DSP ratings are affected by customer complaints and whether they offer a "positive environment" for work.\par \par \uc2 Owners of DSP companies say they are finding it increasingly hard to hit the "fantastic" or "fantastic plus" ratings that trigger bonus payouts. Those additional payments are often the difference between making a profit or losing money for the week, the owners say.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251204elc40001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
