\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Data Centers Are 'Gold Rush' for Workers --- Six-figure pay offered for people in trades, as investment pours into AI infrastructure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DeMond Chambliss used to run himself ragged with the small contracting business he owned in Columbus, Ohio: hanging drywall, chasing clients for payments and managing half-a-dozen employees.\par \par \uc2 Since April, Chambliss has worked the night shift overseeing a team of 200 welders, plumbers and electricians at a local data-center construction site. He makes more than $100,000 a year -- a significant increase from his previous pay -- cruising around on a buggy under floodlights, overseeing deliveries and equipment and ensuring everything stays on schedule.\par \par \uc2 "I pinch myself going to work every day," the 51-year-old said.\par \par \uc2 An investment boom in artificial intelligence is creating a thirst for massive data centers -- and a bonanza for the workers building them. It is unclear how long that boom will last, but for now, workers like Chambliss are cashing in on high demand for their services. They are enjoying the trappings including perks, bonuses and, in many cases, pay boosts.\par \par \uc2 Data centers don't employ many workers once they are actually built. During construction, though, they are a hive of workers pouring concrete walls and foundations, wiring electric panels and installing equipment such as power generators and chillers to ensure servers are cooled to a precise temperature at all times.\par \par \uc2 Given such complexity and high demand, workers who move into the data-center industry -- in roles ranging from electricians to project managers -- often earn 25% to 30% more than they did before, said Jake Rasweiler, senior vice president of data centers at \uc2 Kelly Services\uc2 , a staffing and recruitment firm. "It's like the gold rush," Chambliss said.\par \par \uc2 Data centers are ballooning in size, and a single project can take years to construct and require thousands of workers. Tech giants such as \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  operate 522 data centers and have an additional 411 data centers in development, according to \uc2 Synergy Research Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Hermiston, Ore., Marc Benner, 60, arrives in the predawn hours at a data-center construction site and lines up with scores of workers for a series of synchronized stretches. After that, he spends the day making the rounds ensuring electrical safety. These are lucrative skills at the electricity-gobbling sites, and Benner makes $225,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 "It's my American dream," said Benner, who has been helping build data centers for 15 years, including the ones now powering AI.\par \par \uc2 Clune Construction\uc2 , a Chicago-based general contractor, said it is trying to ensure workers have access to the same creature comforts as white-collar workers, including heated and air-conditioned break tents and perks like periodic free lunches. Around 70% of the company's revenue is from data-center work, a figure that has roughly doubled in the past year.\par \par \uc2 Construction firm Sundt, based in Tempe, Ariz., recently started offering bonuses and paid time off to workers on its sites. Still, the company regularly has to turn down more projects, said Chad Buck, Sundt's president of building.\par \par \uc2 "There aren't enough people to build the magnitude of the work out there right now," Buck said. "There's only a set pool to pull from, and every general contractor in the country is trying to pull from it right now."\par \par \uc2 Construction workers say this heavy demand has created a sense of security that, for them, is often elusive. "In this industry, stability is a really big thing," said Michael Damme, 43, who makes $200,000 a year overseeing concrete construction at three data-center sites for Sundt.\par \par \uc2 "When I first started in the trades, you had to save up all your money in the event you had to sit for a month or two with no work," Damme said. These days, he said, his phone regularly lights up with messages from recruiters eager to lure him to other jobs.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, higher pay is attracting more workers to data-center sites around the country. Shawn Jones, 39, said he makes around $100,000 as a general foreman for \uc2 DPR Construction\uc2  at a data center in Abilene, Texas, around 20% more than what he said he would earn if he stayed home in Colorado. He goes back once or twice a month to see his family, driving 10 hours each way.\par \par \uc2 Some workers don't even have to travel. \uc2 Schweiger Construction\uc2  recently began hiring senior project managers on a remote basis to oversee data-center job sites in places from South Carolina to Indiana. "I don't care where they live," company President Jay Bluhm said. "As long as they're in the continental U.S., and they can fly out where they need to be."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 High Demand Comes Up Against Skills Shortage\par \par \uc2 Demand for workers is colliding with a longstanding shortage of skilled tradespeople that has pinched the construction industry.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Builders and Contractors trade group estimates that the construction industry is short roughly 439,000 workers, mostly among skilled workers who do things such as lay pipe and wire electrical panels.\par \par \uc2 The effects are starting to pile up.\par \par \uc2 A survey by the Uptime Institute of data-center equipment manufacturers, engineers and construction companies found that 52% said staffing shortages on sites caused business disruptions, up from 43% last year. Contractors working on data centers have an average backlog of 10.9 months of work, compared with eight months for their peers, according to data from ABC.\par \par \uc2 "There's an arms race taking place in the world of artificial intelligence," said ABC chief economist \uc2 Anirban Basu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251203elc30000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
