\par\par \b \uc2 USA250: The Business of Work (A Special Report) --- These 10 Books Changed the Way Americans Thought About Work: From Benjamin Franklin to the present, authors have focused on the evolving workplace, and what needs to be done to make it more effective\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carol Hymowitz \par \uc2 1,624 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R25\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It began with Benjamin Franklin, who couldn't stop working -- or writing about work -- throughout his 84-year-long life.\par \par \uc2 Franklin set the stage for thousands of subsequent writers who produced novels, memoirs and nonfiction books focusing on the working lives of Americans. They not only chronicled how the workplace changed as the U.S. evolved from an agrarian to industrial to digital economy, but also had a hand in shaping those changes.\par \par \uc2 Literature about work proliferated partly because of "the fluidity of American labor," says Nancy Koehn, a historian at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2 . In a nation that was an experiment, where people believed they could keep trying new occupations and advancing through work, accounts of efforts to do this have always been popular.\par \par \uc2 Here are 10 of the most influential of these books.\par \par \uc2 1. 'The Autobiography of Benjamin Franklin'\par \par \uc2 by Benjamin Franklin (1791)\par \par \uc2 Often called the consummate "self-made man," Franklin wrote his autobiography in four parts over 19 years. He documented his rise -- from a printer's apprentice when he was 12 to a self-employed printer and publisher, inventor, diplomat and statesman -- blending his personal history with advice about the virtues of work. He never completed the autobiography, and it was published posthumously a year after his death.\par \par \uc2 By then many Americans were deeply familiar with Franklin's maxims about the importance of industriousness, thrift and self-reliance, which he published annually in "The Poor Richard's Almanack" from 1732 to 1757. Among these: "Beware of little expenses; a small leak will sink a great ship"; and "Early to bed early to rise, makes a man healthy, wealthy and wise."\par \par \uc2 Franklin didn't follow all his advice. He loved shopping and wasn't, he admitted, frugal. But he was "cut to the bone with hard labor," wrote Carl Van Doren, one of his biographers, and his "Autobiography" promoted the belief that any American, through diligent labor and discipline, could prosper and advance and keep reinventing himself.\par \par \uc2 2. 'Democracy in America'\par \par \uc2 by Alexis de Tocqueville (1838 and 1840)\par \par \uc2 The French political thinker Tocqueville visited the U.S. in 1831 and published, in two volumes, his observations about how democracy was shaping institutions and daily life. He described Americans as having a "restless" spirit of enterprise, with many frequently changing jobs or moving west in search of new opportunities. Everyone, regardless of their background or status, was expected to work, and manual labor was honorable. This was distinct from Europe where aristocrats lived off inherited wealth, only peasants labored manually and one's place in society was fixed.\par \par \uc2 "Democracy in America" encouraged Americans to see their work ethic as a national character trait. It linked work to mobility and defined work as a civic as well as a moral duty.\par \par \uc2 3. 'Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, a Former Slave'\par \par \uc2 by Frederick Douglass (1845)\par \par \uc2 A firsthand account by an escaped slave who became a famous abolitionist and orator, this memoir reframed slavery as coerced labor. Douglass graphically described the labor he was forced to do -- as a field hand, where he was worked to exhaustion and brutally beaten, and as a shipyard apprentice in Baltimore, where his wages went to his owner.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans, before the book's publication, treated enslaved labor as a necessary and even a natural form of work. Douglass's book, which sold 30,000 copies within five years of its publication, shattered that idea and helped define the "free labor ideal" -- the belief that all people had the right to choose their work and enjoy the fruits of it. This strengthened support for the anti-slavery movement, and Douglass's own life story promoted free labor and self-reliance as a path to independence.\par \par \uc2 4. 'The Jungle'\par \par \uc2 by Upton Sinclair (1906)\par \par \uc2 In 1904, Sinclair spent seven weeks working undercover and gathering information about conditions in the meatpacking plants in Chicago. Telling the story through fictional characters, he first published his findings in serial form for the socialist newspaper Appeal to Reason and then as a book.\par \par \uc2 With its descriptions of cattle with tuberculosis being slaughtered and workers with other diseases handling meat, "The Jungle" sparked an outcry about unsanitary and unsafe conditions in the meat industry. Public pressure led to the passage in 1906 of the Pure Food and Drug Act, which ensured that meatpacking plants processed their products in a sanitary way, and the Meat Inspection Act, which required that the Agriculture Department inspect livestock before slaughter.\par \par \uc2 5. 'Principles of Scientific Management'\par \par \uc2 by Frederick Taylor (1911)\par \par \uc2 Taylor was a steel-plant manager and a mechanical engineer who thought that traditional methods of factory work, where each worker relied on personal skill and custom, were inefficient. His "scientific management" system called for standardization of tasks and managers' oversight of work processes. He urged factory managers to break down each task into its smallest motions, measure these with a stopwatch and then determine the "one best way" to perform the work.\par \par \uc2 Taylor's ideas standardized work, created efficiencies in factories, offices and other workplaces and launched management theory. Critics also say the focus on efficiency eventually became obsessive, and led to workers' mental exhaustion and alienation.\par \par \uc2 6. 'How to Win Friends and Influence People'\par \par \uc2 by Dale Carnegie (1936)\par \par \uc2 Published during the Great Depression when millions were out of work or struggling to hang on to jobs, the book offered practical advice that could be used immediately, with its emphasis on showing a genuine interest in others and avoiding criticism and complaints.\par \par \uc2 Carnegie's central idea -- that success at work depends less on technical knowledge and more on how one relates to people -- aligned with the white-collar, consumer-driven economy emerging in the 1930s. Skills like the ability to persuade others, communicate well and get along with bosses and customers were becoming more important than physical strength. The book helped shape the fields of sales, human resources, public relations and management training. It has sold more than 30 million copies, making it the best known self-help business book.\par \par \uc2 7. 'The Grapes of Wrath'\par \par \uc2 by John Steinbeck (1939)\par \par \uc2 The novel tells the story of a family of Oklahoma tenant farmers who lose their land during the Dust Bowl and migrate to California in search of work and stability. Instead, they face hunger, homelessness and exploitation by large farm growers and vigilantes.\par \par \uc2 The book portrayed migrant workers as victims rather than failures. It was made into a movie starring Henry Fonda, provoking a backlash from agribusinesses and politicians, who accused Steinbeck of exaggeration or promoting socialism. The novel was burned in some California towns. But it also stirred sympathy for migrant farmworkers. It was praised by President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2  and built support for New Deal programs, such as federal migrant camps, and for union-organizing efforts.\par \par \uc2 8. 'White Collar'\par \par \uc2 by C. Wright Mills (1951)\par \par \uc2 The white-collar labor force of office workers and salespeople grew rapidly in America after World War II. These middle-class employees were thought to be more content and comfortable than auto workers or machine operators. Mills, a sociologist, challenged this belief, describing how white-collar work could be just as monotonous, powerless and stressful as factory work. Plus, salaried workers sold not only their time but their personalities -- conforming to corporate codes about how to speak and dress, Mills argued.\par \par \uc2 The book was followed by William H. Whyte's "The Organization Man," published in 1956, which explored how large corporations bred conformity in managers and, as a result, lost their ability to be innovative. Both books entered mainstream discourse, with "the organization man" becoming part of everyday language.\par \par \uc2 9. 'The Feminine Mystique'\par \par \uc2 by Betty Friedan (1963)\par \par \uc2 Credited with helping to ignite second-wave feminism in the 1960s, the book claimed that many middle-class, college-educated women were dissatisfied being confined to their roles as housewives and mothers.\par \par \uc2 The book sold more than three million copies in three years and fueled activism targeting workplace discrimination. Friedan co-founded the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2  in 1966, which lobbied for equal job opportunities, equal pay and for enforcement of Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights Act, prohibiting sex discrimination in employment.\par \par \uc2 "The Feminine Mystique" inspired women to seek and hold on to careers and paid jobs and influenced a sharp increase in women's participation in the labor force.\par \par \uc2 10. 'Nickel and Dimed: On (Not) Getting By in America'\par \par \uc2 by Barbara Ehrenreich (2001)\par \par \uc2 After welfare-reform legislation in the late 1990s pushed millions of American families off public aid and into the workforce, Ehrenreich began investigating what it was like to live on the minimum wage. She took jobs in the low-wage services sector, working first as a waitress in Florida, then a maid in Maine and finally as a retail associate at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  store in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 She quickly realized one job wasn't enough to live on. To afford the cheapest housing and food, she had to take a second job in each location she worked. She still was never able to pay all her bills, let alone save any money or take classes at night to qualify for better jobs.\par \par \uc2 The book bolstered efforts byorganizers to unionize service workers and influenced legislators to seek to increase the minimum wage. It continues today to influence debates about wage inequality and how to address the problems of low-wage workers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Carol Hymowitz is a writer in New York. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251201elc10000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
