\par\par \b \uc2 USA250: The Business of Work (A Special Report) --- Diversity in the Workplace Needs a New Metric: True workplace diversity is about diversity of thought -- and that kind can improve performance\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Edmans \par \uc2 1,012 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FOR DECADES. the corporate world has run with a simple story: Diversity drives performance. Lately, though, the conventional wisdom has been challenged, and for good reason: Independent, peer-reviewed research has found no correlation -- let alone causation -- between diversity and performance.\par \par \uc2 The backlash, though, often conflates flawed implementation with a flawed idea. True workplace diversity isn't about optics -- the way somebody looks -- but is about diversity of thought. According to research I've done for the Diversity Project, a U.K. initiative to improve diversity in asset management, teams perform better when members bring different perspectives, uncover blind spots and interrogate assumptions. This isn't wokeness or virtue-signaling, but better business.\par \par \uc2 Many argue that while hard to measure, cognitive diversity can be approximated by demographics. But the evidence says otherwise: Race, gender and age alone are poor proxies for how people think. Cognitive diversity stems more from differences in education, career paths, political views, problem-solving styles and personality.\par \par \uc2 As is the case so often, easier said than done. How do you get cognitive diversity in a company? Here are some elements to consider:\par \par \uc2 Start with hiring\par \par \uc2 The evidence for cognitive diversity uncovered four types of such diversity that may enhance performance.\par \par \uc2 First, there is professional background -- for instance, across sectors (some asset managers recruit ex-investigative journalists for their information-gathering skills) or across roles, like sales and research.\par \par \uc2 Second, educational background is crucial, making sure hires span arts, sciences, humanities and vocational training.\par \par \uc2 Third, the country of origin can shape geopolitical insight and offer distinct views on politics, culture and global trends.\par \par \uc2 And fourth, cognitive style is also vital, although especially difficult to assess at hiring. Are they intuitive thinkers, super-rational or contemplative? You want a mix.\par \par \uc2 Importantly, the organization should tailor the level and type of cognitive diversity to its business model. Rather than ticking as many boxes as possible, leaders should ask: What perspectives does our team need? Big-picture thinkers and detail-driven executors? Quant jocks and qualitative minds? Optimists and risk-spotters? These traits emerge on the job and can guide team formation.\par \par \uc2 Value dissent\par \par \uc2 Hiring a mix is just the start. Many companies take an "add diversity and stir" approach -- assemble a varied team and wait for magic. But cognitive diversity needs to be actively harnessed.\par \par \uc2 That means being explicit about the value placed on dissent. While this might sound obvious, it runs counter to most corporate norms; junior employees often think their job is to support their manager's position, not question it. That assumption needs to be debunked, and it starts from the top.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Alfred Sloan closed a meeting by asking, "I take it we are all in complete agreement?" Everyone nodded. He continued, "Then, I propose we postpone further discussion of this matter until our next meeting to give ourselves time to develop disagreement."\par \par \uc2 The importance of nudges\par \par \uc2 Small behavioral nudges can have an impact. When forwarding an email, people often include their own take at the top -- but this anchors the recipient before they have seen the original message. Placing it last lets others form their own judgment.\par \par \uc2 The same principle applies in meetings: Bosses should hold back their opinions until others have spoken, and not interject even to express agreement. Even subtle cues -- a raised eyebrow, a nod -- can signal approval or disapproval and steer the room. Encouraging those speaking to state their assumptions, not just their conclusions, helps others challenge the reasoning rather than deferring to seniority.\par \par \uc2 The goal is simple: Give colleagues maximum opportunity to disagree.\par \par \uc2 All ideas aren't equal\par \par \uc2 Cognitive diversity is often taken to mean that everyone should be invited to every meeting in the name of inclusion, and that every opinion deserves equal airtime. The result is bloated discussions, sluggish decisions and diluted expertise. That's "diworsification" -- ideas watered down to the lowest common denominator, the least innovative option that offends no one.\par \par \uc2 So while cognitive diversity only works when people feel safe to challenge, that doesn't mean lowering standards or shielding ideas from critique. In fact, the most innovative teams aren't the ones that avoid conflict -- they're the ones that debate constructively. Nor does it involve creating a "safe space" where no one is ever wrong, just a culture where being wrong doesn't carry a stigma.\par \par \uc2 Inclusion isn't just about who's in the room, but which ideas are heard. It means creating space for uncomfortable conversations and taking seriously opposing views, even those you find objectionable.\par \par \uc2 Where it works best\par \par \uc2 Is there evidence that cognitive diversity actually works? Yes, but it's mixed in two ways. First, it's situation-specific. Cognitive diversity is most valuable in complex, nonroutine tasks, particularly those involving idea generation. But not every meeting is a strategy session. For execution or routine work, homogenous teams often get the job done more efficiently. A good leader knows when to open the floor and when to move fast, rather than treating cognitive diversity as a panacea.\par \par \uc2 Second, even where cognitive diversity helps, the effect is moderate -- because diversity alone isn't enough. It must be harnessed through deliberate actions that amplify its benefits and manage its tensions. In fact, the mixed evidence strengthens the case: If performance gains came simply from hiring a mix of introverts and extroverts, the advantage would be easily replicated. Instead, success hinges on execution.\par \par \uc2 So yes, the original business case for demographic diversity was oversold. But the deeper case for cognitive diversity -- carefully defined, accompanied by real inclusion, and giving priority to minds over metrics -- remains more compelling than ever.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Alex Edmans is professor of finance at \uc2 London Business School\uc2  and author of "May Contain Lies: How Stories, Statistics, and Studies Exploit Our Biases -- And What We Can Do About It." He can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251201elc10000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
