\par\par \b \uc2 USA250: The Business of Work (A Special Report) --- 6 Americans Who Reshaped The Face of Work: From Eli Whitney's cotton gin to Ted Benna's 401(k), these pioneers came up with ideas and innovations that still resonate today\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kerri Anne Renzulli \par \uc2 1,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since America's founding, new ideas and innovations have reshaped what work looks like in this country, as well as how our labor force is compensated and protected while performing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at six of the many people who have driven such changes over the past 250 years.\par \par \uc2 Eli Whitney\par \par \uc2 Eli Whitney had a profound impact on the workplace in two ways. He is best known for his invention of the cotton gin, a machine that transformed cotton production, with tragic consequences for workers. But he also was a gun maker at the forefront of developing machine-made standardized parts, helping to establish a template for factory work in industries worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Whitney's 1793 invention of the cotton gin, a machine that removed seeds easily from the plant's white, fluffy fibers, allowed one person to do the work of dozens of people using their hands. That opened the door to a boom in cotton production, but many more workers would be needed to harvest the crop -- a need that planters filled with a massive expansion of slavery.\par \par \uc2 After Whitney introduced the cotton gin, the number of enslaved people in the U.S. increased from around 700,000 to more than 1.5 million in 1820, says Ryan Paxton, executive director of the Eli Whitney Museum and Workshop.\par \par \uc2 One of Whitney's next ventures was manufacturing muskets for the federal government. Guns at the time were made by skilled craftsmen, limiting their production, and slight variations meant that damaged parts couldn't be easily replaced. To increase production and make repairs quick and easy, Whitney and other gun makers worked toward developing standardized parts that could be easily made and assembled into finished weapons by less-skilled workers.\par \par \uc2 That effort required the invention of new machinery and manufacturing systems, Paxton says. And while Whitney wasn't the first gun maker to create identical, machine-made parts to standardize and speed up production -- what would become known as the American system of manufacturing -- he was one of the pioneers and a champion of the process, which spread to other industries worldwide. "Whitney is really at the forefront of precision manufacturing in this country," says Paxton.\par \par \uc2 Frederick Winslow Taylor\par \par \uc2 Frederick Winslow Taylor was obsessed with efficiency in factories. Some version of the principles he introduced in the late 1880s to achieve that efficiency -- known as scientific management or Taylorism -- have since been widely adopted by nearly every kind of business.\par \par \uc2 "Our world has become so Taylorized, we scarcely see it. It's in every aspect of our lives," says Robert Kanigel, author of "The One Best Way: Frederick Winslow Taylor and the Enigma of Efficiency."\par \par \uc2 Taylor's theories were based on comprehensive studies of people at work. During his time at Midvale Steel in Philadelphia, he felt some laborers purposely worked too slowly. So he decided to break their tasks down to individual actions and measure how long each took, to eliminate unnecessary actions and determine the optimal timing for each task -- so that workers could be held to it.\par \par \uc2 From there he developed the rest of his management system, Some key points: Companies should base their operations on a systematic analysis rather than simply doing things as they've always been done. Incentives should be provided to reward top performers. And employees should be trained in best practices rather than left to figure out their job for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Central to Taylorism is the transfer of control over how work gets done from workers to management. In the 21st century one version of that thinking, Digital Taylorism, is evident at companies that track workers' computers, phones and other technology to be sure the company is getting the most out of its employees.\par \par \uc2 Walter Reuther\par \par \uc2 As the leader of one of the country's largest unions, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , from 1946 until his death in 1970, Walter Reuther improved the earnings and quality of life for the more than 1.5 million employees he represented and millions of other Americans.\par \par \uc2 By pressuring major motor companies like Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler to offer their workers unprecedented benefits, Reuther ensured that laborers got a bigger share of the postwar economic boom American companies were enjoying. That helped workers achieve the largest rise in their standard of living in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 "Reuther shifted the tectonic plates under American capitalism in favor of labor," says Nelson Lichtenstein, a history professor at \uc2 University of California, Santa Barbara\uc2 , and author of "The Most Dangerous Man in Detroit: Walter Reuther and the Fate of American Labor."\par \par \uc2 The 1950 deal Reuther negotiated with GM, dubbed the Treaty of Detroit, set a standard for labor-management relations, becoming the blueprint unions used for future agreements across industries for decades. Through it and other deals Reuther negotiated, workers gained extensive unemployment benefits, company-subsidized medical care, expanded vacation time and fully funded pensions -- and cost-of-living adjustments to wages, a first in a mass-production industry.\par \par \uc2 Reuther also used his union's might to fight for a more just society. He protested alongside \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\uc2 , helped organize and finance the 1963 March on Washington, and was instrumental in gathering support for the passage of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Voting Rights Act of 1965, the Fair Housing Act, and Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 A. Philip Randolph\par \par \uc2 One of the most prominent voices for social and economic justice in the mid-20th century, A Philip Randolph's calls for action compelled presidents to dismantle laws enforcing discrimination and racial segregation in the workplace and to enact new ones promoting equality.\par \par \uc2 As the head of the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters, Randolph believed in the power of strikes and direct action to get results. Using the threat of a mass March on Washington in 1941, he pressured President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2  to enact an executive order that banned ethnic and racial discrimination in the country's defense industries and established the Fair Employment Practice Committee. It was the first executive civil-rights directive since Reconstruction, and the first federal act to promote equal opportunity in the workplace and prohibit employment discrimination.\par \par \uc2 "Randolph recognized that Roosevelt needed the support of Black workers and that he was vulnerable to pressure because he wanted to preserve the unification of the American people during World War II," says Clayborne Carson, founder of the Martin Luther King Jr. Research and Education Institute at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 . "He decided to use this as an opportunity to gain Black workers greater job opportunities and equality."\par \par \uc2 Following World War II, Randolph co-founded the League for Nonviolent Civil Disobedience Against Military Segregation, which encouraged men to refuse to register or serve in the armed forces. Its efforts helped spur President \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  to issue executive orders that prohibited discrimination in federal employment and ended segregation in the armed services -- at the time, the most far-reaching antidiscrimination measures since the Reconstruction era.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the 1950s and '60s, Randolph fought for broader federal changes. In 1963, his vision of a mass march on Washington, D.C., was realized with the assistance of Bayard Rustin. The political rally helped push Congress to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which outlawed discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex and national origin, and gave all Americans greater employment opportunities and protections.\par \par \uc2 Esther Peterson\par \par \uc2 As the powerhouse behind the Equal Pay Act of 1963, Esther Peterson helped raise the wages of working women, ensuring that, in at least some cases, they received the same compensation as men for the same work.\par \par \uc2 A year after the Equal Pay Act, Title VII of the Civil Rights Act passed, banning sex-based discrimination in hiring, firing and promotion. Together, these two pieces of legislation made illegal many forms of sex discrimination that were not only commonly accepted in the U.S. but also in some cases written into the legal code. State laws at the time mandated different minimum wages for women, and union contracts outlined separate pay schedules for the same job depending on sex.\par \par \uc2 While the Chamber of Commerce and other business groups fiercely opposed the bill, its supporters felt raising pay stimulated economic growth and eliminated unfair competition, ultimately benefiting men as well as women, says \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  history and labor-studies professor emerita Dorothy Sue Cobble, author of "For the Many: American Feminists and the Global Fight for Democratic Equality."\par \par \uc2 In her role as head of the Women's Bureau in the Labor Department during the Kennedy administration, Peterson established the President's Commission on the Status of Women to study the challenges working women faced and make recommendations to address them.\par \par \uc2 The commission's report, which came out a few months after the passage of the Equal Pay Act, highlighted the concentration of women in lower-wage work, the toll of domestic labor inside the home and the double discrimination that minority women dealt with. It advocated for universal child care and paid leave for medical and parental needs -- issues still debated.\par \par \uc2 The report's insights sparked a debate over the value of women's work and reinvigorated the feminist movement as different groups used it to advocate for change. Peterson, meanwhile, established the first on-site daycare center at a federal government agency, to support fellow employees.\par \par \uc2 Ted Benna\par \par \uc2 In 1980, Ted Benna convinced his employer at the time, Johnson Cos., to set up the first 401(k) retirement-savings plan, which it put in place the following year. Today, more than 70 million Americans have stashed more than $9 trillion in these tax-advantaged accounts, according to the \uc2 Investment Company Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Benna's inspiration for the 401(k) stemmed from his unique interpretation of a little-noticed 1978 addition to the tax code that allowed employers to offer their staff the choice of accepting bonus payments in cash or deferring them by depositing them, untaxed, into a retirement plan. Benna found that nothing in the code prevented the tax-deferral provision from being applied to regular wages, too. Nor did it prevent employers from putting in matching contributions to encourage staff to participate.\par \par \uc2 Many doubted the feasibility of Benna's idea until the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  issued new regulations in 1981 that made it clear that employers could legally deduct a portion of workers' wages to be contributed to these plans. With \uc2 IRS\uc2  approval, the 401(k) savings plan Benna envisioned skyrocketed in popularity, quickly becoming widespread.\par \par \uc2 "The most valuable thing the 401(k) has done is help turn spenders into savers," says Benna. "Most people, myself included, would have never saved the amount they did if it hadn't been taken directly from their paychecks."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Kerri Anne Renzulli is a writer in Orlando, Fla., and London. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251201elc10000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
