\par\par \b \uc2 USA250: The Business of Work (A Special Report) --- Help Wanted: The Changing Face of Job Listings From the 1790s to Now: Old classified ads reveal a lot about the nature of labor -- and the economy -- since the founding of the country\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heidi Mitchell \par \uc2 1,538 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Remember classified ads? They may have been supplanted by digital job boards, but for much of the country's history, they were one of the main sources to find work and workers. And they reveal a lot about the American experience.\par \par \uc2 The earliest known "want ad," according to Ilana Gershon, a professor of anthropology at \uc2 Rice University\uc2 , was placed in Williamsburg's Virginia Gazette on Feb. 24, 1752. It read: "A Sober Person, well recommended, who hath been us'd to the Employment of an Oysterman on York River, may meet with good Encouragement, on applying to Benjamin Bryan."\par \par \uc2 As time passed, newspapers became the primary source of job listings. And while most job postings have moved online, clues of what was taking shape in the labor market can still be found in the breadcrumbs left behind in old postings.\par \par \uc2 After the Revolution (1783-90s)\par \par \uc2 The Virginia Gazette (Williamsburg, Va.), April 16, 1779\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. economy shifted from colonial mercantilism to building its own markets, the want ads were dominated by land, shipping and artisan trades. Slave owners, prevalent in the South, would lend out their human chattel for project work, as with this ad from James Anderson, seeking Black men to work as laborers for six months in exchange for 100 British pounds per slave -- given to their owner.\par \par \uc2 Anderson is also in want of a blacksmith and nailer to be paid wages as a foreman, which would therefore make them white. "Here you see an early example of the racialized division of labor in the workplace by 'skill' and supervision, correlated with slave labor and free labor," says Alex Lichtenstein, professor of history and American studies at \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gershon also sees something else in this advertisement: "This ad is an example of an early, and much darker, version of contract work," she says. "Not every slave worked directly for their owner."\par \par \uc2 Seeking wage labor (1815-40s)\par \par \uc2 The New Hampshire Gazette (Portsmouth, N.H.), Aug. 7, 1827\par \par \uc2 As America expanded by building canals, steamboats and early railroads, demand soared for wage labor in factories, transportation and among office workers like clerks. That, says Gershon, led to the rise of human-resources departments. "Railroads needed a form of standardization and high-level coordination across towns and stations for specific skills," she says.\par \par \uc2 Signs of industry (1840s-60s)\par \par \uc2 Daily National Democrat (Marysville, Calif.), Oct. 4, 1860\par \par \uc2 As America moved to mechanized textile mills, telegraphs and urbanization, that created demand for factory workers, machinists and communications clerks. This also gave rise to more "intelligence offices," which were essentially private, unregulated employment agencies that connected workers to jobs. The problem, for many, was that there was no guarantee of a job placement, but laborers using these critical agencies nonetheless had to pay an upfront (often exorbitant) fee, which led to easy exploitation.\par \par \uc2 The agencies were so vulnerable to corruption, says Gershon, that city- and state-based labor bureaus began to emerge and unions began to create their version of intelligence offices.\par \par \uc2 Civil War economy (1861-65)\par \par \uc2 The Daily Dispatch (Richmond, Va.), Nov. 18, 1863\par \par \uc2 Wartime production, rail expansion and emancipation were reshaping labor markets. Freed men were accepting wage work in the North, but in the South, slaves were still being "hired out" (i.e., rented) for cash. "This was one way of shifting enslaved labor from agriculture to industry, a necessity for the confederacy during the Civil War," says Lichtenstein.\par \par \uc2 But that could be costly, Gershon says. "When you have slaves, you have to feed them, clothe them, house them safe from the 'enemy.' You had a specific set of obligations as an owner of humans," she says. "Aside from all the moral objections, it was a very inefficient form of hiring temporary workers."\par \par \uc2 An industrial boom (1870s-90s)\par \par \uc2 The New York Herald, May 16, 1875\par \par \uc2 The rise of steel, oil and railroads led to massive immigration to industrial centers, along with a surge in help-wanted ads for factory hands, railroad workers and related jobs.\par \par \uc2 Around 1875, classified pages began clustering lots of jobs on one page. "This concentration of ads into one area of a newspaper dovetails with the same moment when workers could no longer rely solely on their connections to get a job," says Gershon.\par \par \uc2 Hiring volumes of people in one go was logistically difficult. Trying to determine who among the applicants had "good moral character" without knowing their families made it even trickier. Letters of reference and well-crafted cover letters became critical.\par \par \uc2 Panic and recovery (1890s-1900s)\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republican (Phoenix), Aug. 28, 1909\par \par \uc2 The Panic of 1893 was followed by a boom in corporations and their demand for what we now call white-collar office workers, such as stenographers and bookkeepers. This ad required applicants to apply with references through "the Hub," which is likely an HR department. Though it's not made explicit in this ad, during this era, filing cabinets and carbon-copy machines became standard, which meant that jobs previously held by men with good handwriting were shifted to women trained as typists.\par \par \uc2 "A stenographer was a new job, and it led to women joining the workforce -- though many only remained employed until they got married," says Gershon.\par \par \uc2 Urban development (1900-14)\par \par \uc2 The Detroit Times, March 14, 1914\par \par \uc2 Ford invented the assembly line -- and urban growth exploded. Ads for factory hands, salesmen and machinists took off, while old jobs, like nailers, faded into distant memories. "This has always been the history of technology and jobs," says Gershon. "The question is, does the new technology create as many as it eliminates? It will produce different ones -- like telephone operators -- but not all forms of automation are accompanied by the same volume and quality of the job." Being a nailer, she says, is probably a better job than being a factory worker producing nails.\par \par \uc2 World War I economy (1914-18)\par \par \uc2 Evening Public Ledger (Philadelphia), Aug. 26, 1918\par \par \uc2 War mobilization created labor shortages, resulting in more women and Black workers joining the industrial workforce. Gershon sees this as a period when factory owners were clearly trying to think about additional benefits that they could supply, beyond just the wage. "To me these ads say, if you are imagining yourself as working in this plant, you will be responsible for your own food, but we will provide opportunity for you to buy your own food," she says.\par \par \uc2 The employment office also mentions that transportation will be provided, a new concern as "commuting" is normalized. Lichtenstein notes that this phenomenon is of particular concern for women, especially those -- as is the case here -- working the night shift. "As women moved into the industrial workforce, employers often sought to assure the public that they were 'protecting' them from the dangers of public life," he says.\par \par \uc2 Great Depression and New Deal (1930s and 1940s)\par \par \uc2 Mass unemployment and scarce private hiring led to the Works Progress Administration (WPA), which was part of FDR's New Deal. This also marks a time when American workers gained rights never before seen, such as Social Security. "This changes the ways in which people think about their future and their retirement," says Gershon. "This is a moment when people are reimagining the contract between the company or the government and its workers."\par \par \uc2 Postwar boom (1946-60s)\par \par \uc2 After the war, manufacturing boomed, the GI Bill fueled white-collar growth and workers fanned out to the suburbs. All those engineers, teachers and clerical staff sought jobs far beyond their traditional networks, which led to employers increasingly asking for resumes. Meanwhile, companies had to offer benefits packages, which typically included health insurance and vacation time, to remain competitive.\par \par \uc2 Information economy (1980s-90s)\par \par \uc2 Deregulation, globalization and personal computers transformed the concept of office work. Ads for information technology workers, finance, sales and other "knowledge economy" jobs fueled the economy, and advertising moved online with the launch of \uc2 CareerBuilder\uc2 , HotJobs, Monster and others. Even after the frothy bubble popped in the early 2000s, digital job boards had supplanted the centuries-old classified.\par \par \uc2 All this digital career searching meant workers had to be willing to publicly post their resumes, which could make them vulnerable at their current jobs.\par \par \uc2 The rise of the gig economy (2008-20)\par \par \uc2 With the collapse of housing and finance markets and the global financial crisis, ads declined sharply. When the economy rebounded, growth was most acute in technology and healthcare roles -- along with "gig" work. What was once a pejorative term -- "moonlighting" -- morphed into a more-attractive "side hustle."\par \par \uc2 The AI revolution (2023-present)\par \par \uc2 It's all happening fast, but artificial intelligence is predicted to upend the job market. Researchers Hamid Ekbia and Bonnie Nardi point out that with every new technology, there is a change in heteromation, or in how humans and machines divide up tasks, says Gershon. "That is happening now."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Heidi Mitchell is a writer in Connecticut and London. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 (See accompanying illustrations -- WSJ Dec. 1, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251201elc100005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
