\par\par \b \uc2 USA250: The Business of Work (A Special Report) --- A Timeline of Key Moments in Work in America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Louis Hyman \par \uc2 2,628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Early Republic\par \par \uc2 (1776-1820)\par \par \uc2 1776: Independence and the Labor Paradox\par \par \uc2 The Declaration proclaimed all men created equal, yet the new nation's economy depended on enslaved labor in the South and indentured servitude in the North. This contradiction would shape American work for a century. Most Americans worked in family units -- the household, not the factory, was the center of production.\par \par \uc2 1793-1820s: Cotton Slavery\par \par \uc2 Eli Whitney's cotton gin expanded slavery across the Deep South. The enslaved population grew from 700,000 in 1790 to nearly 1.5 million by 1820. Planters operated the nation's first "big businesses," managing large labor forces through factory-like regimentation. Meanwhile, Northern states began gradual emancipation, making slavery a sectional institution dividing North from South.\par \par \uc2 Market Revolution\par \par \uc2 (1820-60)\par \par \uc2 1828: The 10-Hour Movement\par \par \uc2 The 10-hour workday struggle became the defining labor cause of the early republic. The movement exploded in 1828 as workers resisted longer workdays, invoking the Declaration's "pursuit of happiness." In 1831-33, New York ship carpenters won the first major American victory in reducing work hours. The movement believed economic progress should free humans from marketplace dependence -- that freedom meant leisure, not endless labor.\par \par \uc2 1808-60: End of Atlantic Slave Trade\par \par \uc2 While Congress banned slave importation in 1808, it didn't ban the buying and selling of people. The domestic slave trade exploded as the Upper South sold people to cotton and sugar plantations, financed by London banks. By 1860, hundreds of thousands had been moved southward, tearing apart families while fueling the Cotton Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 1822-40s: Mill Girls\par \par \uc2 The Boston Associates built Lowell, Mass., in 1822, creating a distinctly American industrialization. Young farm women lived in boardinghouses, working 12-14 hour days for cash wages. The system was explicitly temporary. Though analogies were drawn to household production, the reality differed: Work was repetitive, boring, without break -- the machines never stopped.\par \par \uc2 1820s-45: Apprenticeship's Decline\par \par \uc2 The apprenticeship system collapsed as masters hired day workers rather than training apprentices. Young men became wage workers rather than future craftsmen. In Lynn, Mass., shopkeepers consolidated skilled labor while distributing semiskilled work to households. Independent masters articulated grievances through "producerism" -- the belief that labor created all value and merchants were parasites.\par \par \uc2 1820-60: Native American Wage Labor\par \par \uc2 As U.S. expansion displaced indigenous nations, Native peoples entered wage labor, in ways that often extended traditional work. Tongva, Chumash and Cahuilla workers in California formed the backbone of rancho agriculture. Across the Great Lakes, Ojibwe and Menomini worked lumber camps. Cheyenne hunters supplied buffalo robes to trading companies.\par \par \uc2 1840s-50s: Domestic Service And Clerks\par \par \uc2 Domestic service was the largest urban occupation at midcentury, dominated by Irish immigrant women who received room and board and could save significant amounts. Meanwhile, white-collar clerkships grew sharply -- by 1855, clerks were New York's third-largest occupation, entrusted mostly to native-born men. The counting house trained the next generation of merchants, creating a distinct white-collar class.\par \par \uc2 1860: Slavery at Its Peak\par \par \uc2 By 1860, nearly four million enslaved people produced cotton, sugar, rice and tobacco -- the most-profitable form of labor in America. Nearly 80% of Louisiana sugar mills relied on steam engines, making it the most heavily capitalized agricultural region. Northern bankers financed slave mortgages and plantation finances; Northern manufacturers sold cloth and shoes for enslaved workers.\par \par \uc2 Civil War and Reconstruction\par \par \uc2 (1860-77)\par \par \uc2 1861-65: War and Emancipation\par \par \uc2 The Civil War mobilized unprecedented production, shifting the North toward full industrialization. Women entered the industrial workforce by tens of thousands. Most important, the Emancipation Proclamation and 13th Amendment freed millions of enslaved people. Former slaves sought to work for themselves and negotiate wages. Former enslavers sought ways to twist the law to force freedpeople back to their lands through labor contracts, convict leasing and debt peonage.\par \par \uc2 1865: Chinese Railroad Workers\par \par \uc2 Between 10,000 and 12,000 Chinese workers labored on the transcontinental Central Pacific Railroad -- the largest corporate wage-labor force in 19th-century America. Most were recruited from southern China's Pearl River Delta.\par \par \uc2 1877: The Great Railroad Strike\par \par \uc2 On July 16, 1877, Baltimore & Ohio workers struck to protest wage cuts. The strike spread across states and turned violent. Federal troops killed over 100 workers. In St. Louis, strike committees, the so-called St. Louis Commune, briefly governed before troops crushed the uprising. The strike marked the first nationwide U.S. strike and beginning of Industrial Age labor unrest.\par \par \uc2 1870s-90s: First Deindustrialization\par \par \uc2 As textile production shifted from Massachusetts's Merrimack River to the Piedmont plateau of the Appalachian foothills. New England experienced America's first deindustrialization. Investors and machinery moved south seeking cheaper labor. In the South, entire families worked long hours for subsistence wages in company-owned villages. This pattern of capital flight would recur throughout U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 Industrial Age\par \par \uc2 (1877-1913)\par \par \uc2 1869-90s: The Knights of Labor And the AFL\par \par \uc2 The Knights of Labor, founded in 1869, became the first mass labor organization, welcoming all "producers": skilled and unskilled, Black and white, men and women. At its 1886 height, the Knights claimed nearly one million members, seeking a cooperative commonwealth transcending the wage system. Following the backlash to the Haymarket Square bombing and a series of failed strikes, the Knights faded. In December 1886, Samuel Gompers founded the American Federation of Labor, organizing only skilled workers and focusing on "pure and simple" unionism that rejected state intervention.\par \par \uc2 1892-94: Homestead and Pullman Strikes\par \par \uc2 When industrialist Henry Frick moved to break the union at Carnegie's Homestead, Pa., plant in July 1892, skilled workers resisted. Pennsylvania sent 8,000 Guardsmen to break the strike, replacing skilled steelworkers wherever possible with machines. In 1894, Pullman workers struck over wage cuts. Socialist labor leader Eugene Debs led a national boycott only to have it broken by federal troops. The willingness of the state to use force to break strikes proved a cautionary tale.\par \par \uc2 1890-1930: The Office and the Feminization of Work\par \par \uc2 The expanding corporate bureaucracy created a vast demand for white-collar labor. Between 1900 and 1930, female typists and stenographers increased more than 10-fold. Women's clerical employment exploded from 687,000 in 1910 to 4.5 million by 1950. Though more respectable than factory work, pay remained low and advancement rare. Industrial discipline migrated from shop floor to office desk.\par \par \uc2 Progressive Era\par \par \uc2 (1900-20)\par \par \uc2 1912: Bread and Roses\par \par \uc2 More than 20,000 immigrant workers from 51 nationalities struck in Lawrence, Mass., when mill owners cut pay. Under IWW organizers, "Bread and Roses" became the first successful interracial, cross-ethnic industrial strike in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 1911-17: Triangle Fire and WWI Labor Laws\par \par \uc2 The Triangle Shirtwaist fire in New York killed 146 workers in March 1911, leading to workplace safety laws and the Labor Department's formation. During World War I, the Adamson Act set an eight-hour workday for railroad workers -- the first federal law regulating private-sector hours. The Supreme Court accepted that government could regulate hours. Congress prohibited child labor under age 14. Wartime labor shortages began the Great Migration of African-Americans from the South.\par \par \uc2 1917-19: Red Scare\par \par \uc2 After WWI, American radicals found hope in the Russian Revolution. Some 350,000 steelworkers walked off in the Great Steel Strike. The Red Scare followed -- the government arrested and deported radicals, breaking unions like the IWW and hobbling labor for a decade.\par \par \uc2 The Interwar\par \par \uc2 (1920-40)\par \par \uc2 1920s: Rolling Back Protections\par \par \uc2 After WWI, progressive labor protections were rolled back. The Supreme Court struck down the Child Labor Act (1918) and minimum wage for women in 1923. Employers attacked unions through the "American Plan."\par \par \uc2 1929-33: Mass Unemployment\par \par \uc2 The Great Depression threw 15 million out of work with 25% unemployment by 1933. Unemployed WWI veterans marched on Washington in 1932 to demand bonuses, only to have their encampments dispersed with tanks and tear gas. The Depression marked the first time unemployment was seen as structural crisis rather than personal failure.\par \par \uc2 1933-35: NIRA and the Wagner Act\par \par \uc2 Roosevelt's National Industrial Recovery Act granted workers the right to collectively bargain. \uc2 United Mine Workers\uc2  leader John L. Lewis announced: "The president wants you to unionize." The \uc2 UMW\uc2  grew from 100,000 to 400,000 members. When the Supreme Court ruled NIRA unconstitutional in 1935, Roosevelt signed the Wagner Act, guaranteeing collective bargaining. Union leaders formed the Congress of Industrial Organizations, organizing mass-production industries.\par \par \uc2 1936: Sit-Down Strikes\par \par \uc2 Workers at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  occupied factories for 44 days in Flint, controlling irreplaceable auto body dies. Michigan's governor, in a reversal of the historical pattern, refused to use militia to retake the plant, forcing \uc2 GM\uc2  to recognize the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1937: Court-Packing and Reversal\par \par \uc2 Frustrated by his programs being ruled unconstitutional, FDR threatened to "pack" the Supreme Court by adding justices. But in the "switch in time that saved nine," the high court reversed course, upholding minimum wage. The court also upheld the Wagner Act, broadly expanding federal regulatory power.\par \par \uc2 1938: Fair Labor Standards Act\par \par \uc2 On June 25, 1938, Roosevelt signed the FLSA, banning child labor and setting a federal minimum wage (25 cents an hour) and maximum 44-hour workweek.\par \par \uc2 World War II And Postwar Accord\par \par \uc2 (1940-70)\par \par \uc2 1940-45: War Production\par \par \uc2 War production ended unemployment, creating 17 million jobs. More than five million women entered high-wage manufacturing. Roosevelt banned racial discrimination in defense work after Randolph threatened a March on Washington. The Bracero Program began importing Mexican guestworkers in 1942 to replace American farmworkers. Expanding wartime industries, like aerospace, were unionized in return for no-strike pledges.\par \par \uc2 1945-47: Taft-Hartley\par \par \uc2 After Victory Day, 4.6 million workers struck in the largest strike wave in American history. Congress overrode Truman's veto to enact Taft-Hartley on June 23, 1947, restricting unions by banning closed shops, secondary boycotts and sympathy strikes, allowing "right-to-work" laws and requiring union officers to disavow communism.\par \par \uc2 1950: Treaty of Detroit\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  and \uc2 GM\uc2  signed a five-year contract linking wages to productivity and cost-of-living. This "Treaty of Detroit" became the postwar model -- unions accepted management control in exchange for rising wages and benefits.\par \par \uc2 1940-70: Native Industrial Labor\par \par \uc2 WWII drew indigenous workers into mines, logging and defense production. After 1945, the BIA's Relocation Program moved families into cities for low-wage manufacturing. By the 1960s, reservation electronics plants recruited Native women for circuit-board work with the promise of "self-help" development.\par \par \uc2 1955-56: \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  Merger\par \par \uc2 The AFL and CIO merged in 1955. By 1956, white-collar workers outnumbered blue-collar workers for the first time -- marking the transition to a service economy.\par \par \uc2 1959: Union Corruption\par \par \uc2 After the McClellan Committee exposed wrongdoing by Teamster leaders, Congress passed the Landrum-Griffin Act on Sept. 14, 1959. While addressing corruption, it further subjected unions to government oversight.\par \par \uc2 1962-68: Public Sector Unions And Farm Workers\par \par \uc2 Kennedy's Executive Order 10988 gave federal employees bargaining rights. Public unions quickly grew and by the 1970s, the public sector was the most unionized sector. In 1968, civil-rights leader \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\uc2  was assassinated while marching with Memphis garbage workers. Cesar Chavez and \uc2 Dolores Huerta\uc2  organized farmworkers in 1962. Their grape boycott won contracts in 1970, but excluded from FLSA protections and facing INS pressures, farmworkers remained largely unorganized.\par \par \uc2 1963-64: Equal Pay Act and Title VII\par \par \uc2 Kennedy's Equal Pay Act mandated equal pay by sex but excluded domestic workers, agricultural workers and executives. Johnson's Civil Rights Act created Title VII, prohibiting employment discrimination. Labor strategy shifted from collective union action to individual legal remedies.\par \par \uc2 1971: \uc2 OSHA\par \par \uc2 The Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  was created to enforce workplace safety standards, shifting safety from union negotiation to government regulation.\par \par \uc2 The Neoliberal Turn\par \par \uc2 (1970-Present)\par \par \uc2 1971: Undocumented Industry\par \par \uc2 The 1970s integrated undocumented Mexican workers into industrial America. INS data revealed two-thirds of apprehended workers labored in industrial and construction jobs, not agriculture as conventionally believed. California's 1971 employer sanctions proved toothless.\par \par \uc2 1975: Free Agency in Baseball\par \par \uc2 An arbitrator ends the "reserve clause" that had tethered players to one employer for life (and had earlier been challenged by Curt Flood), triggering the largest salary gains of any group of employees, anywhere -- and changing labor practices in all U.S. sports.\par \par \uc2 1981: Patco Strike Broken\par \par \uc2 More than 12,000 air-traffic controllers struck in August 1981. Reagan fired them on Aug. 5, banned them from federal employment, and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  decertified the union. This was the first time since before the New Deal that the government broke a union, signaling a new era. Strike rates plummeted as employers felt free to permanently replace strikers.\par \par \uc2 1982: Capital Moves\par \par \uc2 Factory closures devastated Northern and Midwestern cities as companies moved South and overseas. Unemployment reached 9.5% in 1982. This second industrialization coincided with containerized globalization. In 1982, \uc2 GM\uc2  demanded givebacks; the union gave in, ending cost-of-living adjustments. \uc2 GM\uc2  shut down three plants anyway. Strikes hit their lowest level since WWII.\par \par \uc2 1984-93: Silicon Valley and Nafta\par \par \uc2 Politicians ignored Silicon Valley jobs where automation language hid a workforce of immigrants, often women, frequently undocumented. Workers remained unorganized. The \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  in 1993 accelerated manufacturing job losses. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  became America's largest employer with a low-wage, antiunion model, depressing services sector wages.\par \par \uc2 2001: Algorithmic Workforces\par \par \uc2 New algorithmic scheduling software optimized for profits rather than employee needs, subjecting workers to uncertain scheduling and volatile income. Software would cap schedules at 29 hours to keep workers part-time and ineligible for benefits, accelerating precarity for shift workers in retail, warehousing and food service.\par \par \uc2 2007-09: Recession\par \par \uc2 The recession revealed decades of stagnation. Americans had concealed this through increased female workforce participation and rising house prices that created an illusion of wealth as wages flatlined.\par \par \uc2 2009: Gig Work\par \par \uc2 Smartphone apps created "gig work" -- \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 Instacart\uc2 . Companies classified workers as independent contractors, avoiding minimum wage and benefits -- the culmination of decades of pushing risk onto workers.\par \par \uc2 2012: Fight for $15\par \par \uc2 On Nov. 29, 2012, approximately 200 fast-food workers walked off across New York City, demanding $15 per hour and the right to unionize. Coming after Occupy Wall Street, the Fight for $15 tapped into concern about inequality. Though funded by unions, participants didn't belong to unions. The movement looked to state legislatures to pass higher minimum-wage laws.\par \par \uc2 2018-19: New Workers\par \par \uc2 California passed AB5 in 2019 requiring companies to classify workers as employees rather than independent contractors, but \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  spent over $200 million on Proposition 22 to exempt themselves, which passed in 2020. Teachers in West Virginia, Oklahoma and Arizona walked out, demanding better pay. These strikes and regulations showed renewed labor militancy.\par \par \uc2 2020-21: Remote Work And Essential Workers\par \par \uc2 The pandemic and lockdowns revealed which workers were "essential" -- including healthcare workers, grocery clerks, delivery drivers, meatpacking workers. These largely low-wage workers kept going in to work while many white-collar workers shifted to remote work, exposing inequalities.\par \par \uc2 2021-2025: Organizing This New Economy\par \par \uc2 Workers quit in record numbers, seeking better pay and work-life balance. The tight labor market gave workers leverage they hadn't had in decades. Workers at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  organized unions. Writers Guild and \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2  struck simultaneously in 2023 over streaming residuals and artificial-intelligence protections, winning important AI protections. By the 2020s, contingent workers constituted a growing share of the workforce. The stable, full-time job with benefits had become increasingly rare, especially for younger workers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Louis Hyman is an American political-economy historian and professor at Johns Hopkins. He can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251201elc100001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
