\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Labor-Market, Inflation Concerns Persist\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The job market continues to sputter even as cost and price pressures persist, a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  report found, underscoring the competing risks central bank officials will have to weigh at a key meeting in December.\par \par \uc2 Employers showed little appetite for hiring, which fell in half of the Fed's 12 regional districts, while the recently ended federal government shutdown and artificial intelligence also weighed on job seekers' prospects, according to the "beige book," an anecdotal look at the economy, gleaned from surveys and interviews by each of the Fed's branches.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, tariffs and health insurance added to business costs, potentially fueling inflation concerns, the beige book found.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is split on what to do at its Dec. 9-10 meeting, between officials who prefer a third-straight quarter-point rate cut because of the slowing labor market, and those who want to hold rates steady at 3.75% to 4% because of elevated inflation.\par \par \uc2 Markets currently put an 85% probability on a cut, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The beige book could be more important to their decision than usual because so much data has been suspended because of the recent government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Here are five takeaways from the November beige book, published Wednesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Job market sputters\par \par \uc2 Over the past six weeks, there wasn't much positive momentum for job seekers. Employers' appetite for hiring fell in half of the Fed's 12 regional divisions, the central bank said.\par \par \uc2 Across most of the country, companies said it is getting easier to find the workers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  said that in its region covering parts of six Southeastern states, many businesses were either cutting staff or only hiring as many people as needed to replace departing workers.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2 's district, which covers Ohio and parts of Pennsylvania and Kentucky, some retailers said they reduced staffing because sales had slowed.\par \par \uc2 Inflation still stubborn\par \par \uc2 The Fed noted that manufacturers and retailers were facing higher input costs that they blamed on tariffs.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\uc2 ' district, covering parts of the Midwest and Northern Plains, a brewery reported that the cost of aluminum cans is up.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, companies have been trying to pass on those costs to their customers, boosting consumer inflation.\par \par \uc2 In others, companies have been eating those costs and accepting lower profits, the Fed said.\par \par \uc2 But other forces have also been driving up costs: Many companies in multiple districts noted, for instance, that the rising cost of providing healthcare for workers is straining budgets.\par \par \uc2 Consumers pull back\par \par \uc2 Wealthier Americans continued to drive solid sales at higher-end retailers, the beige book found, but otherwise, consumer spending has been under pressure.\par \par \uc2 Many companies said that shoppers are less willing now to accept higher prices as household budgets tighten.\par \par \uc2 Car dealers noted that fewer people were shelling out for electric vehicles after federal tax subsidies expired.\par \par \uc2 One business told the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City\uc2  that it was a great time to get a tattoo, because top artists who are usually booked up suddenly have openings on their schedules.\par \par \uc2 Shutdown took a toll\par \par \uc2 The fallout of the record-long government shutdown that began last month didn't only hit federal workers.\par \par \uc2 Government employees who were off the job pulled back which, for example, led to a drop in sales at Pennsylvania auto dealers, the \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  noted.\par \par \uc2 But the shutdown also hit the economy in other ways. Airports in the Midwest saw a decline in commerce as travel snarls kept travelers away.\par \par \uc2 And charity organizations said they faced higher demand during a disruption in government nutrition benefits.\par \par \uc2 AI making its mark\par \par \uc2 AI led to booming investment in some areas -- and a pullback in hiring in others.\par \par \uc2 A representative manufacturer in New England told the \uc2 Boston Fed\uc2  that orders grew thanks to surging demand for AI-related infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 But the technology is also transforming the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Some companies said they were pulling back on entry-level hiring because they are leaning more on AI to do basic tasks.\par \par \uc2 One higher-education contact in the Boston region reported that fewer students wanted to study fields such as data science out of concern that AI will replace those jobs.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251128elbs00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
