\par\par \b \uc2 HP\uc2  to Slash Workforce in AI Push --- The computer and printer maker says 10% cut to affect 4,000 to 6,000 jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kelly Cloonan \par \uc2 494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HP\uc2  plans to cut up to 10% of its workforce as it invests further in artificial intelligence, a move its chief executive sees as essential to maintaining its competitive edge.\par \par \uc2 The computer and printer maker estimates about 4,000 to 6,000 employees will be affected by the end of fiscal 2028. It employed about 58,000 people as of its latest annual filing.\par \par \uc2 In after-hours trading, \uc2 HP\uc2  shares fell as much as 5%.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Enrique Lores\uc2  said the restructuring plan is part of a push to adopt AI across the company to accelerate product and software development as well as automate customer support and some internal processes.\par \par \uc2 "We really think this is a unique opportunity we cannot miss to really continue to transform the company and continue to be competitive for the next 10, 20 years," Lores said.\par \par \uc2 While cutting jobs in some departments as it adopts AI internally, \uc2 HP\uc2  also expects to increase investment in some areas to integrate the technology further into its product portfolio, Lores said.\par \par \uc2 "I think any work is going to be impacted by AI, and we need to take advantage of it as a company," Lores said.\par \par \uc2 HP\uc2  expects to incur about $650 million in costs related to the restructuring plan, including about $250 million in the current fiscal year. It expects the actions will result in annual savings of at least $1 billion by the end of fiscal 2028.\par \par \uc2 The move also comes as \uc2 HP\uc2  faces higher costs in its personal-computers business as a surge in AI demand from data centers drives up the price of memory chips.\par \par \uc2 The company plans to raise the prices of its computers, work with lower-cost suppliers and reduce memory configurations to partially offset those costs, though still expects a net headwind of 30 cents on earnings a share for the current fiscal year, Lores said.\par \par \uc2 "As you can see, it is a very significant number, and this is net of all the actions that we are already putting in place," Lores said.\par \par \uc2 For the current fiscal year, \uc2 HP\uc2  projects adjusted earnings per share of $2.90 to $3.20, behind analyst estimates of $3.34, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the fiscal first quarter, the company expects adjusted earnings per share of 73 cents to 81 cents. Analysts forecast 78 cents.\par \par \uc2 For its latest quarter, \uc2 HP\uc2  posted a profit of $795 million, or 84 cents a share, compared with $906 million, or 93 cents a share, a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Fourth-quarter adjusted earnings per share were 93 cents, ahead of estimates of 92 cents a share.\par \par \uc2 Revenue rose 4.2% to $14.64 billion, topping analyst estimates of $14.5 billion. Personal Systems revenue rose 8% to $10.35 billion, offsetting a decline in printing sales, down 4% to $4.27 billion.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251126elbq0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
