\par\par \b \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's Results Fail to Quell AI Worries --- Tech juggernaut's earnings sparked a furious rally at first and then a slump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Wirz \par \uc2 846 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 's best wasn't good enough to rescue the increasingly wobbly stock market on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Blockbuster\uc2  results from the artificial-intelligence bellwether sparked a furious rally from Tokyo to New York before indexes reversed course and declined in the largest blown gain since April's tariff-fueled market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 The sharp move extended a painful stretch for the stocks at the heart of the AI boom. It demonstrated how on edge investors remain over tech companies' swollen valuations and aggressive spending plans, which some fear mark signs of a bubble.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the worries on Thursday: A shutdown-delayed jobs report ended a weekslong blackout on key economic data, but did little to clarify the state of the labor market and the potential for a long-debated December interest-rate cut from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For a while, investors cheered the dissipating fog, with the Nasdaq composite surging as much as 2.6%. Then tech shares slumped, driving the index to a 2.2% decline. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  climbed as much as 5%, before finishing the session down about 3%. Bitcoin slumped anew, to its lowest 4 p.m. level since April. The Cboe Volatility Index, known as Wall Street's "fear gauge," rose around 12%.\par \par \uc2 "What stands out to me is the lack of any substantial shift in narrative to cause such a big shift," said \uc2 Tony Roth\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Wilmington Trust Investment Advisors\uc2 . "There's just not a lot of confidence in the market right now."\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 closed 1.6% lower, after initially jumping 1.9%. The Dow Jones Industrial Average swung more than 1,000 points, surrendering a more than 700-point gain and losing 0.8% to close 387 points lower.\par \par \uc2 The losses deepened in Asia early Friday. Stock markets in Japan, Hong Kong and Shanghai were all down more than 2% at midday.\par \par \uc2 One factor behind the moves: The much-awaited release of the September jobs report, postponed during the government shutdown, showed the U.S. added 119,000 positions, beating consensus forecasts. However, the unemployment rate unexpectedly ticked up to 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Some investors said the numbers were stale and contradictory, giving little guidance on what to expect from a deeply divided Fed next month.\par \par \uc2 Traders had scaled back rate-cut bets when the Labor Department said October employment data wouldn't be published until mid-December, after the central bank's next meeting. That reflected investors' views that policymakers would need more evidence of a weakening labor market to justify a cut.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate futures suggested investors now see a nearly 40% chance that Fed officials will cut rates in December, according to \uc2 CME\uc2  data. That is up from 30% on Wednesday, but down from around 99% a month ago. The yield on the 10-year Treasury note fell to 4.105% on Thursday, from 4.132% on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The stock selloff came after \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  reported a 62% increase in sales of AI data-center chips and increased its guidance for the current quarter. In recent sessions, investors punished big tech companies, worried that some are spending too much on data centers, chips and other AI infrastructure with little hope of recouping their investments anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also increasingly jittery about how tech giants are using large and complex debt deals to finance the infrastructure build-out. The cost of swaps that protect holders against default in bonds issued by \uc2 Oracle\uc2  -- a big supplier of computing power to AI labs -- jumped by nearly 50% since mid-October, according to data provider Solve.\par \par \uc2 Other stocks tied to the AI trade fell too. \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  dropped 11%, extending its weekly loss to 18%. \uc2 Western Digital\uc2  slid 8.9%, and \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2  retreated 7.8%.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Nvidia\uc2  is a leader in the market but the AI ecosystem is beyond \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 ," said \uc2 Omar Aguilar\uc2 , chief investment officer at Schwab Asset Management. "The credit market seems to be providing a barometer that there may be risks on the horizon."\par \par \uc2 And declines in everything from industrial firms to bitcoin suggested a broad retreat from risk. At 4 p.m., the largest cryptocurrency was at $86,337, down more than 30% from a peak above $126,000 set on Oct. 6.\par \par \uc2 Bright spots in the market included \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , which rose 6.5% to lead gains in the Dow industrials and S&P 500 after the U.S.'s largest retailer reported robust earnings and raised its annual outlook.\par \par \uc2 With the corporate earnings season drawing to a close and no major economic data releases expected ahead of the Fed's meeting, the next market signposts remain unclear. That uncertainty might be encouraging large institutional investors to trim their holdings to lock in gains from earlier in the year.\par \par \uc2 Individual investors also showed signs of ebbing confidence. Retail investors were net sellers of single stocks this week, unloading about $728 million worth, according to research by \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich448\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6720
