\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Jobs Report Set to Come Out Next Thursday\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry and Matt Grossman \par \uc2 192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The reopened federal government will next Thursday release the first backlogged monthly jobs report, for the month of September. And a flood of other data is due to start flowing soon.\par \par \uc2 There is pent-up demand for new numbers after the longest shutdown on record -- topping six weeks -- delayed a critical batch of indicators that economists, analysts and investors track closely to judge the health of the American economy. These include federal reports on the job market, inflation, economic growth and spending.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  announced the revised schedule Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The jobs data are especially important because the health of a shaky U.S. labor market has become a growing concern. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have been divided during the data blackout over whether persistent inflation or the sluggish labor market is the greater economic threat, clouding the path for interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The latest monthly jobs report, published Sept. 5, showed that employers created 22,000 new jobs in August and that the unemployment rate ticked up to 4.3%.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251115elbf0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
