\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: What to Know as the Government Reopens --- Legislation signed by Trump ensures back pay and reversal of firings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Thomas and Sabrina Siddiqui \par \uc2 730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is reopening after a record-setting, 43-day shutdown. Now that President Trump has signed legislation to fund the government, the return to normal is expected to come relatively quickly -- though mountains of work await federal workers.\par \par \uc2 Here is what to know.\par \par \uc2 Pay timing\par \par \uc2 If the reopening follows past shutdowns, federal workers should be paid within days. Officials said the timing will vary based on the federal agency involved. Military servicemembers continued to receive their pay during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 The bill reverses the thousands of firings of federal workers started by the Trump administration during the shutdown. It also ensures back pay for federal workers. The White House had questioned whether that money was guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 The legislation refers to laws that secure back pay for workers "at the earliest date possible after the lapse in appropriations ends, regardless of scheduled pay dates." Some agencies, such as the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and the State Department, signaled they would move swiftly to issue back pay to employees, according to people familiar with their plans.\par \par \uc2 Reopening\par \par \uc2 Federal agencies should begin resuming their activities, though the restart might vary agency by agency. For example, a State Department spokesman said that once the government reopens, the department expects to "immediately resume operations, including returning to normal staffing levels, activities, and programming," and that employees will return to work the next business day.\par \par \uc2 HHS\uc2  employees were told to be prepared to return to work Thursday once Trump signs the bill.\par \par \uc2 Once they return, federal workers will have to play catch-up. "All of their work will have been piling up while they have been gone," said \uc2 Max Stier\uc2 , the president and chief executive of the \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2 , a nonpartisan group that promotes an effective civil service. "It's going to be a mess."\par \par \uc2 In January 2019, Trump signed the bill to reopen the government on a Friday. By the following Monday, most federal agencies were reopening, and federal employees were returning to work. A Jan. 25, 2019, memo from \uc2 Russ Vought\uc2 , Trump's first-term budget chief, who has returned to the role in his second term, instructed executive departments and agency heads to "reopen offices in a prompt and orderly manner."\par \par \uc2 Air travel\par \par \uc2 The end of the government shutdown doesn't mean travelers will immediately escape the specter of flight cancellations and delays. It will be days -- perhaps longer -- before U.S. air travel snaps back to normal.\par \par \uc2 Airlines need time to adjust schedules after the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  required a reduction in the volume of flights at 40 airports. Low air-traffic-control staffing weighed on the \uc2 FAA\uc2  before the shutdown, and that problem is likely to persist in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 SNAP funding\par \par \uc2 Many of the nation's roughly 42 million Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program recipients have been in limbo since the Trump administration declined to finance benefits in full during the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 At least 16 states paid out November benefits in full after a federal judge in Rhode Island ruled that the federal government must fully fund SNAP payments. Many other states were waiting for the matter to be settled in court as the Trump administration appealed the decision, leaving millions across the country who rely on the program without food assistance.\par \par \uc2 Once the government reopens, how quickly SNAP funding will be available to states that haven't distributed payments is hard to determine. It depends on how soon the administration makes those funds available and how long it takes states to disburse them, according to food-aid groups. Some states might release the money immediately, while others might experience a delay while getting their payment systems back on track.\par \par \uc2 Parks and museums\par \par \uc2 Most national parks around the nation, including Yellowstone and Yosemite, have stayed partially open, staffed by a skeleton crew of rangers and other federal workers.\par \par \uc2 Once the government reopens, visitors can expect to be charged entrance fees at the parks again, and park employees will need to assess damage and clean up.\par \par \uc2 Smithsonian museums and the National Zoo have been closed since mid-October. According to the Smithsonian's records, more than 60% of its annual funding comes from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251114elbe0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
