\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Boeing Union Workers Approve New Contract --- The nearly 15-week strike by machinists had curbed jet-fighter production\b0\par\par\uc2 By Drew FitzGerald \par \uc2 299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's unionized machinists ratified a new collective-bargaining agreement Thursday, ending a nearly 15-week strike that took a toll on workers responsible for producing jet fighters.\par \par \uc2 Employees in the regional union chapter, IAM 837, rejected several earlier company proposals before they approved the latest offer. The contract raises average annual base pay to about $109,000 from $75,000 over its five-year term. The company also cut some proposed incentive payments in return for a $6,000-per-worker signing bonus.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Midwestern machinists help assemble F-15 fighters, trainer aircraft and munitions at three factories in the St. Louis region. The aerospace giant's production rate is expected to surge in the coming years after it builds a new production line for the \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2 's next-generation F-47 fighter fleet.\par \par \uc2 "We're pleased with the results and look forward to bringing our full team back together on Nov. 17 to support our customers," the company said.\par \par \uc2 The union said some of its late-shift members would go back to work as soon as Sunday evening.\par \par \uc2 The new agreement, covering about 3,200 workers, settles a standoff that had grown more bitter by the day since union members walked off the job in early August. Rank-and-file workers had bucked union leaders' endorsements of earlier offers.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  leaned on managers and other nonunion workers during the strike and in recent weeks said it had hired replacement workers.\par \par \uc2 The new contract's wage bump will increase labor costs for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  by $20 million a year on average, according to \uc2 Sheila Kahyaoglu\uc2 , an aerospace and defense analyst at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251114elbe00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
