\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: U.S. Says October Economic Data Likely Won't Be Released\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry and Matt Grossman \par \uc2 495 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two major government reports on inflation and the labor market for October are "likely never" to be released, the White House press secretary said.\par \par \uc2 The six-week government shutdown largely halted the release of government data that Wall Street and economic policymakers rely on to measure the economy's health. The House of Representatives approved and President Trump signed a bill to end the shutdown Wednesday night, but the White House indicated the reports on inflation and employment for last month will be lost due to the long closure of federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 "All of that economic data released will be permanently impaired, leaving our policymakers at the Fed flying blind at a critical period," Karoline Leavitt told reporters Wednesday, regarding the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leavitt said the government shutdown, which she blamed on Democrats, "made it extraordinarily difficult for economists, investors and policymakers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to receive critical government data."\par \par \uc2 The White House declined to provide more information about Leavitt's statement.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  hasn't yet said when it is likely to start catching up on the backlog of important economic reports, or which ones might be compromised by the shutdown. A \uc2 BLS\uc2  spokesperson didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Leavitt has previously said that October consumer-inflation data were in jeopardy because of the shutdown. Wednesday was the first time the White House called the October jobs report into question.\par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report is among the most market-sensitive indicators the government produces. The recent lack of official economic reports has sent economists and investors scrambling for other clues, including a spate of private-sector reports.\par \par \uc2 These reports suggest the U.S. is still gripped by weak hiring, and potentially an increase in layoffs, underscored by the cutting of tens of thousands of white-collar jobs. Thousands of government workers who took the Trump administration's deferred resignations only came off payroll in October, a factor expected to weigh on the overall jobs number for the month.\par \par \uc2 In August, the last month for which federal data was available, the unemployment rate was 4.3% and employers added 22,000 new jobs -- continuing a sharp slowing in job creation.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  is expected to quickly release the backlogged September jobs report after the government reopens, since it is pegged to data collected before the closure began on Oct. 1. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  had intended to release those numbers on Oct. 3.\par \par \uc2 The October data present a tougher challenge because the shutdown left government workers unable to collect the stats.\par \par \uc2 Some of those figures, such as on job creation and wholesale prices, could be salvageable because companies provide a portion of it directly to \uc2 BLS\uc2 . But other important metrics, such as consumer prices, could prove difficult to reconstruct in retrospect, according to analysts who track the government statistics system.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251113elbd0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
