\par\par \b \uc2 Technology & Business: \uc2 Synopsys\uc2  to Cut Workforce by 10%, Close Sites\b0\par\par\uc2 By Connor Hart \par \uc2 249 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Synopsys\uc2  will lay off about 10% of its workforce and close certain sites as part of a restructuring plan that the company said would allow it to invest in the business and drive efficiencies following its recent acquisition of \uc2 Ansys\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are taking a number of targeted steps to improve our efficiency to scale the business, accelerate our strategy and capitalize on the highest-growth opportunities," the chip-software-design company said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Most of the job cuts will occur during \uc2 Synopsys\uc2 ' fiscal 2026, which began Nov. 1, the Sunnyvale, Calif., company said. It expects to substantially complete the restructuring by late 2027.\par \par \uc2 Synopsys\uc2  declined to share the number of layoffs it expects to conduct. The company had about 20,000 employees at the end of its fiscal 2024, according to its most recent annual filing with the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 . \uc2 Ansys\uc2  employed 6,500 people as of the end of last year, regulatory filings show.\par \par \uc2 Synopsys\uc2  said it expects to record as much as $350 million in pretax charges stemming from the restructuring, mostly consisting of severance and other one-time termination benefits. Some of the costs will additionally stem from site closures, it said.\par \par \uc2 The restructuring plan follows \uc2 Synopsys\uc2 ' $35 billion cash-and-stock deal for \uc2 Ansys\uc2 . The deal, which was announced in early 2024 and closed in July, expanded the company's reach in simulation software for designers of microchips, cars and airplanes.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251113elbd00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*