\par\par \b \uc2 Technology (A Special Report) --- \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's CFO on the Power of Getting Personal: \uc2 Sarah Friar\uc2  talks about how the company believes it can take advantage of AI's personalization capabilities, while avoiding some of the risks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Krouse \par \uc2 1,218 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AI's rapid expansion has raised all sorts of profound questions for users, from the mundane to the existential. Two of the most immediate ones: Will AI destroy a lot of jobs, and how to avoid some of the risks of AI's ability to get personal?\par \par \uc2 Wall Street Journal tech and media editor Sarah Krouse spoke with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chief financial officer, \uc2 Sarah Friar\uc2 , to get insights into how the AI giant views those two questions. Here are edited excerpts of their conversation at the 2025 WSJ Tech Live conference in Napa Valley, Calif. (\uc2 News Corp\uc2 , the parent company of The Wall Street Journal, has a content deal with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 WSJ: Is enterprise adoption of AI replacing jobs? There are headlines every week of white-collar jobs going away and mass layoffs, often under the banner of AI.\par \par \uc2 FRIAR: There are areas like customer success where there's a lot of that. Chatbots can do without humans in the loop. But there are a lot of other places where AI is accelerating human workloads.\par \par \uc2 Inside my own finance team, AI lets us get rid of a lot of the more mundane, backward-looking, analysis type jobs, and we're moving people much more quickly into an insight job.\par \par \uc2 I'll give you an example. Every CFO has to deal with something called the flux. You get your actual financial data in, and then you look at what you expected. You compare across the two, across hundreds of line items.\par \par \uc2 Someone's job, usually junior accountants, is to look for where there's a bigger deviation than expected. And then they go off and they call people and say, "Why is performance marketing up 300%? Is it a mistake? If it's real, what's happening?"\par \par \uc2 On my team, we now have an AI agent that does that whole flux for us. It trawls through and says, here's all the things that look a little out of the norm. It goes back into \uc2 Slack\uc2  and into our \uc2 Google\uc2  Docs and so on, and it will say, "Oh, looks like there was a meeting about performance marketing where we decided to go bigger in this country and that's why it's up 300%. So this looks reasonable."\par \par \uc2 Then we can have a conversation to say, "OK, marketing team, was that performance marketing successful? Should we do more and if so, should we budget more for it?" Suddenly, you're having really good conversations with the business about how the business can go faster, instead of being the historians of the company.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: There's a lot of exuberance around AI and AI spending. Do you worry that some of the exuberance will fade out?\par \par \uc2 FRIAR: I don't know if there's enough exuberance about AI when I look at the actual practical implications and what it can do for individuals.\par \par \uc2 Think about a mom with a diabetic kid who's at her wit's end about what to make for dinner tonight. Chat just changes the whole paradigm because of the ideas that you can get from it. The ability to photograph your fridge and immediately have a menu plan.\par \par \uc2 Or a cancer patient can get maybe drugs that were never thought of before. A model was able to come up with a new molecular compound and a distribution mechanism that gets it to the cancer, and gets you through \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval multiples times faster than we could even imagine. So, the exuberance is real, and we should keep running at it.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: It feels like a lot of the world that you're envisioning for consumers is intense personalization. We've seen some of the harm that can come from that. We've seen tragic events, people taking their own lives. How do you measure the risk of harm that your products might pose and manage that?\par \par \uc2 FRIAR: All of those elements are very front and center internally. We've tried to be very transparent to the outside as well. For example, when we saw the model was starting to get very sycophantic, we pulled it back, and we wrote a very long blog post about what we saw.\par \par \uc2 When we think about the underlying first priorities of where we're going with chat, No. 1 is definitely memory, because we think this adds an incredible amount of utility to the consumer. If I'm working with \uc2 Booking.com\uc2  or \uc2 Expedia\uc2 , chat has the memory of knowing where I've been on vacation or that I'm a family of four. We like to stay in houses, not hotels. All of that just cuts to the chase so much more quickly, so I have a better experience.\par \par \uc2 I always think about when \uc2 Uber\uc2  went public, they went public with this tagline about being everyone's private driver -- something only really affluent people had experienced, the black car that showed up for you, was now available to everyone. When I think about that same thing for ChatGPT, I think about a world of where everyone can now have a concierge doctor, everyone can have a personal tutor, everyone can have a stylist, you can have a travel agent, you can have all of these things. Now we need to get better, I think, at manifesting it.\par \par \uc2 Study and Learn mode, to me is the first example of when we turn ChatGPT into a tutor for you. We're doing something much more than just telling it to be smart. So if I turn on Study and Learn mode and say, "Tell me about plants," the first thing it will probably do is ask you a question back.\par \par \uc2 Kind of a classic teacher move, right? Socratic methodology. "Tell me what you know about plants," and then it might tell you a little bit and say, "OK, does that make sense?" Or, "Where else do you want to go with this conversation?" As you go further, it will test to see if you're learning now.\par \par \uc2 With all of that, we need to make sure we're not addicting people into the app. You can do a lot, even just with your own personalizing of the app, to remind yourself that we're all human in the end, and human interaction is also very healthy for us all.\par \par \uc2 WSJ: But we essentially have to say, "Help yourself to my calendar, my \uc2 Slack\uc2 , my full life." That's a lot for people to hand over to you guys.\par \par \uc2 FRIAR: It is, although remember that when they're doing it within a personal context, but particularly in an enterprise context, that's all done with full enterprise security.\par \par \uc2 I can only get access to the things that I'm allowed access to. As a CFO, I can see most things in the organization, but maybe some things are still not open to me. That data is not leaving your enterprise. The security is very, very tight because corporates are not going to deploy this if they don't believe you've hit that bar.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251112elbc00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
