\par\par \b \uc2 Israeli Spyware Firm Gets New Owners\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dov Lieber \par \uc2 982 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV -- \uc2 NSO Group\uc2 , the Israeli company behind Pegasus spyware, said a group of investors led by Hollywood producer Robert Simonds has acquired a controlling stake in the firm, which has named a former Trump official to lead an effort to restore its battered reputation.\par \par \uc2 The company, which has faced lawsuits and \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  sanctions since revelations that its technology was used to spy on political dissidents, human-rights advocates, journalists and American officials, declined to disclose the purchase price.\par \par \uc2 NSO\uc2 's new executive chairman, \uc2 David Friedman\uc2 , a former U.S. ambassador to Israel and onetime bankruptcy lawyer for President Trump, said he wants to use his ties to the Trump administration to help rebuild the company's spyware business in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "If the administration, as I expect they'll be, is receptive to considering any opportunity that might keep Americans safer, it will consider us," said Friedman.\par \par \uc2 Friedman and \uc2 NSO\uc2  said Simonds, the chairman of STX Entertainment, declined to comment for this article. Friedman said he and Simonds want to use \uc2 NSO\uc2 's eavesdropping and surveillance technologies to "achieve a safer world."\par \par \uc2 NSO\uc2 's flagship product, Pegasus, has used \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  to infiltrate phones without the target having to do or tap on anything. The spyware has also been sent to phones via links in messages, according to security researchers.\par \par \uc2 Pegasus can turn a smartphone into a silent spying device by gaining access to its files, messages, microphone and camera, they say.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the Biden administration placed \uc2 NSO\uc2  on an export-prohibition list that restricted the firm from obtaining some types of technology from the U.S. In 2023, President Biden signed an executive order banning government agencies and departments from using commercial spyware that "poses risks to national security or has been misused by foreign actors to enable human rights abuses around the world."\par \par \uc2 Unless Biden's executive order is rescinded, it is unlikely \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agencies would do business with \uc2 NSO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since its legal troubles began, the company has struggled financially and changed hands a few times. It mulled pivoting away from spyware but says it has since refocused on cyber offensive capabilities and Pegasus remains the company's key product.\par \par \uc2 "They have been struggling and looking for capital. This change of control and ownership brings with it sufficient capital to maintain the business," Friedman said.\par \par \uc2 Intelligence agencies such as the \uc2 U.S. National Security Agency\uc2  and the U.K.'s Government Communications Headquarters routinely use hacking tools. \uc2 NSO\uc2  often sells such cyber capabilities to countries that don't have their own.\par \par \uc2 NSO\uc2 's products would be "additive" to existing technologies used by U.S. law-enforcement and intelligence agencies, Friedman said. He said he is aiming to recruit U.S. law-enforcement agencies, including police forces, among the company's customers.\par \par \uc2 In March 2022, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  said in congressional testimony that the bureau had bought \uc2 NSO Group\uc2  technology to test it and didn't use it for operational purposes.\par \par \uc2 As ambassador to Israel, Friedman oversaw the U.S. recognition of Jerusalem as Israel's capital, the relocation of the U.S. Embassy to Jerusalem and the negotiation of the Abraham Accords, a series of diplomatic normalization agreements between Israel and some Muslim-majority countries.\par \par \uc2 Friedman said his pitch to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is that \uc2 NSO\uc2 's products will make America safer. \uc2 NSO\uc2  says its products can be used by government agencies to fight terrorism and crime by allowing them access to encrypted messaging systems such as \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company says it doesn't know who its clients target with Pegasus. But it said it terminates contracts when it learns of abuses and has taken measures to stem abuse and vet potential clients. Friedman said preventing abuses would come down to finding trustworthy clients.\par \par \uc2 The power of Pegasus creates "unbearable temptation for abuse," said \uc2 John Scott-Railton\uc2 , a researcher with the Citizen Lab, an academic research group that studies state-sponsored cyber espionage.\par \par \uc2 "I can't think of something more chilling for Americans' basic rights and freedom than some police department reaching into their lives and dumping it on the table in front of a bunch of cops," said Scott-Railton.\par \par \uc2 He said that despite vows by the company to prevent abuse, cases continue to emerge. Two journalists from the Balkan Investigative Reporting Network, a Serbian network of investigative journalists, recently received links on the Viber messaging app infected with Pegasus, according to researchers at \uc2 Amnesty International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Friedman said "the \uc2 NSO\uc2  of today is a far more careful company in how it licenses its technology than it was five six years ago."\par \par \uc2 From 2020 through 2024, \uc2 NSO\uc2  spent $7.6 million on lobbying efforts in Washington, according to Open Secrets, a government transparency group that tracks political spending.\par \par \uc2 Those efforts bore little fruit, said Steve Feldstein, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\uc2  in Washington. He said the fact that \uc2 NSO\uc2 's technology was used to spy on U.S. officials abroad turned opposition to the company into a bipartisan issue.\par \par \uc2 NSO\uc2  still faces significant legal hurdles. In 2019, \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 's parent company, now called \uc2 Meta\uc2 , sued \uc2 NSO\uc2  over what it alleged was a breach of its servers to install \uc2 NSO\uc2 's malware on target devices. In July, the six-year trial came to an end, with a federal jury in California ordering \uc2 NSO\uc2  to pay Meta $168 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 In October, the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California reduced the fine \uc2 NSO\uc2  was ordered to pay \uc2 Meta\uc2  to $4 million. But in the same ruling, the judge ordered \uc2 NSO\uc2  to stop targeting \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 , in a move that the company said during its defense could put it out of business.\par \par \uc2 NSO\uc2  is appealing the decision against targeting \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 , and is filing for a stay.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251110elba0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
