\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Use AI, Or You're Fired --- At companies big and small, employees have feared being replaced by AI. The new threat: Being replaced by someone who knows AI.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lindsay Ellis \par \uc2 1,604 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Julie Sweet\uc2 , the chief executive of consulting giant \uc2 Accenture\uc2 , recently delivered some tough news: \uc2 Accenture\uc2  is "exiting" employees who aren't getting the hang of using AI at work.\par \par \uc2 The firm has trained about 70% of its roughly 779,000 employees in generative artificial-intelligence fundamentals, she told investors. But employees for whom "reskilling, based on our experience, is not a viable path" will be shown the door, Sweet said.\par \par \uc2 Rank-and-file employees across corporate America have grown worried over the past few years about being replaced by AI. Something else is happening now: AI is costing workers their jobs if their bosses believe they aren't embracing the technology fast enough.\par \par \uc2 From professional-services firms to technology companies, employers are pushing their staffs to learn generative AI and integrate programs like ChatGPT, Gemini or customized company-specific tools into their work. They're sometimes using sticks rather than carrots. Anyone deemed untrainable or seen as dragging their feet risks being weeded out of hiring processes, marked down in performance reviews or laid off.\par \par \uc2 Companies are putting their workers on notice about their AI skills amid a wave of white-collar job cuts. \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  announced layoffs in late October that affected roughly 14,000 jobs, while Target recently shed 1,800 corporate roles. \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2  has also disclosed thousands of cuts. Executives at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 IBM\uc2  have tied workforce cuts to the technology in statements this year.\par \par \uc2 Some companies are training people in how to use the tools -- but leaving it up to them to figure out what to use them for. There are countless possibilities for how to deploy AI. Some businesses have required training classes or set up help desks to coach employees on how to incorporate AI into their work. Others are putting the onus on staff to think creatively about how to make money or save time with the tech.\par \par \uc2 That can prompt exciting innovations -- or it may come at the expense of getting work done. Or both.\par \par \uc2 At enterprise-software company IgniteTech, leaders required staff last year to devote 20% of their workweek to experimenting with AI. On one such "AI Monday," staff brainstormed ways to speed up processes like automating responding to customer-service tickets. Employees also had to share on \uc2 Slack\uc2  and X what they were learning about AI.\par \par \uc2 CEO Eric Vaughan said that employees self-assessed their AI usage and, afterward, the company used ChatGPT to rank the results. After a human review, IgniteTech cut the lowest-scoring performers.\par \par \uc2 "By their own admission, they're in the basement," he said. "So now they have to leave."\par \par \uc2 It wasn't easy: Vaughan recalls speaking with his wife over that time about the changes, feeling "terrible." But he said he felt AI was an existential threat, and that if IgniteTech didn't transform, the company would die. One tough exit was the chief product officer, who had been with the company for years. He and others were model, productive employees historically but were resisting the AI mandate, said Vaughan, who also leads \uc2 GFI Software\uc2  and Khoros.\par \par \uc2 Greg Coyle, that executive, said he had bought into AI's potential to improve IgniteTech's products and add new capabilities. But he took issue with the nature of the widespread cuts, particularly because the technology is in such an early stage.\par \par \uc2 "Doing this rapid culling of your workforce, it's very risky," he said. "If your AI plan doesn't work out the way you expected it to, it's a huge risk for the business."\par \par \uc2 After a round of cuts, Coyle said he pushed back against an AI mandate in late 2023 in an executive meeting. He said he felt the company wasn't working strategically as it pushed out staff. A few months later, he said, he was fired.\par \par \uc2 AI, Coyle said, is "coming whether we like it or not. You either get on board or you get left behind." But, he added, "I don't believe that you take this brute force, across-the-board approach to AI in the business."\par \par \uc2 Vaughan said the company has since hired AI specialists to replace the laid-off staff. \uc2 Accenture\uc2  has said that it expects to increase headcount this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 At workforces large and small, plenty of workers are hesitant to adopt AI, fearful that widespread adoption will innovate them out of a job. They also doubt the technology can do the job as well as they can.\par \par \uc2 A recent Gallup survey found that more than 40% of U.S. workers who don't use AI say the main reason is they don't believe it can help their work. A smaller share, 11%, said their primary driver was that they did not want to change how they worked. While AI adoption has grown in the past year, working Americans are about three times as likely to say they aren't prepared at all for AI as opposed to "very prepared," Gallup found.\par \par \uc2 Many employees, even when exposed to AI tools that companies spend a lot on, aren't biting. When researchers at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2  reviewed more than 300 AI initiatives, they found only 5% were achieving quantifiable value. Employees flock to tools like ChatGPT and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Copilot for their ease of use, but don't often adopt other software.\par \par \uc2 A big impediment, the researchers found, is that many of those tools aren't yet programmed to learn from users' past interactions. That makes approaching a human colleague a better option for complex work. The best return on investment, the researchers found, has often been on back-office functions.\par \par \uc2 Companies are finding other ways to push staff to integrate AI into their work.\par \par \uc2 At McKinsey, analytic problem solving is at the heart of what consultants do. When that skill is measured in future performance reviews, consultants will be evaluated on how they make decisions with AI. Now, in assigning staff to some client projects, McKinsey gives priority to employees who are trained in AI, said \uc2 Kate Smaje\uc2 , a senior partner and global leader of technology and AI.\par \par \uc2 People in \uc2 KPMG\uc2 's human-resources division are assessed on how well they collaborate with AI in their wider evaluations, the firm's head of people said.\par \par \uc2 PwC is requiring AI training for its newest hires. It kicked off a nine-piece pilot curriculum for new-graduate associate hires in October, including lessons on "prompting with purpose," designing workflows that include AI and instruction on how to use the tools responsibly.\par \par \uc2 And at a fall PwC all-partner meeting with thousands of attendees, working with the technology was part of the agenda. The multimillion dollar investment in AI training "will absolutely pay off," said Margaret Burke, the firm's head of recruiting and learning and development.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Concentrix\uc2 , a customer-service outsourcing company with more than 400,000 staff, bosses recently realized low-performing developers weren't using AI.\par \par \uc2 "You find out those people are refusing to adjust," said Ryan Peterson, \uc2 Concentrix\uc2 's chief product officer.\par \par \uc2 Concentrix\uc2  hired Peterson from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  in 2024 with a mandate to find ways to incorporate AI across the company. Its attorneys now use AI to redline new versions of contracts. The technology flags clauses that the company would never agree to in negotiations -- like accepting unlimited liability, Peterson said. These efficiencies mean that \uc2 Concentrix\uc2  was able to redeploy 10 attorneys to higher-value negotiation work and litigation management.\par \par \uc2 Purchasing teams use the technology to compare requests for proposals, and marketing teams now use it to format and template emails, he said.\par \par \uc2 Concentrix\uc2 's CEO said in a June earnings call that he doesn't foresee a "massive decrease" in employment, though he noted that declining head count is a possibility.\par \par \uc2 Multiverse, an education-tech company in London, states that its mission is to advance AI adoption. Each quarter, it awards an employee who has come up with the best uses for AI 10,000 pounds, or about $13,000. Finalists this quarter include the creator of a paperwork automation system that cut a 30-minute task to five minutes and someone who made a sales aide that creates a customized briefing based on publicly available information.\par \par \uc2 Job applicants at Multiverse are asked in interviews how they use AI in their lives, and in one assignment, prospective hires write prompts to complete certain tasks, said Libby Dangoor, who oversees the company's human resources and AI among other areas. If applicants are skeptical of AI, it would be picked up in the application process, she said.\par \par \uc2 "We have to hire for AI will, not just skill," she said.\par \par \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  job postings requiring AI literacy skills have expanded by 70% in the 12 months ended in July, according to the site.\par \par \uc2 Annie Hamburgen, 28, of Incline Village, Nev., left her marketing job in March to travel in South America. When she came back and began looking for new work this summer, prospective employers kept asking her about AI. "I've been trying to demonstrate my openness to learning while making it clear that I'm not going to blindly type things in and accept whatever result comes out," she said.\par \par \uc2 Hamburgen recently got hired for a role leading integrated marketing and starts on Monday. In conversations with her future boss, it's been clear that she should be using AI to synthesize information. A common refrain: "Type it into Grok!"\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251108elb80001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
