\par\par \b \uc2 Finance: Goldman Promotes 638 Employees to Managing Director\b0\par\par\uc2 By AnnaMaria Andriotis \par \uc2 398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  on Thursday promoted 638 employees to the role of managing director, a bigger group than the last class two years ago.\par \par \uc2 The managing director role is the last stop before employees can ascend to Goldman's partnership, and typically comes with a base salary of around $400,000. That doesn't include bonuses, which can range from thousands to millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Year-end bonuses across much of Wall Street are expected to be up from 2024, according to an analysis by Johnson Associates, in large part because big banks are raking in bigger profits from their trading and investment banking divisions.\par \par \uc2 The managing director title at Goldman also comes with access to the bank's leadership training program, private investment funds and a personal wealth adviser. New classes of managing directors and partners are named in alternating years. In 2023, Goldman named 608 employees to its class of managing directors.\par \par \uc2 More than 70% of the new managing director class comes from revenue-producing divisions, Goldman said.\par \par \uc2 Goldman has been putting a renewed focus on its core Wall Street divisions of investment banking and trading, as well as its asset- and wealth-management businesses.\par \par \uc2 Goldman's senior executives have also made it a priority to increase the firm's lending, part of a quest to boost revenue streams from recurring fees. The goal is twofold: to help offset the firm's more volatile trading and dealmaking businesses and to extract revenue from clients in more ways.\par \par \uc2 Goldman said 27% of the new managing directors are women. That is down from 31% in its previous class, and the lowest share of the new classes in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The firm also said 3% and 4% of the new managing directors are Black and Hispanic, respectively. In the previous class, 2% were Black and 4% were Hispanic.\par \par \uc2 More than 6,000 interviews with partners and managing directors occurred since September as part of Goldman's process to determine which employees should be promoted in the new class.\par \par \uc2 New managing directors in India account for 7% of the new class, up from 5% of the previous class. The firm has been expanding its presence in the country, mostly with a focus on back-office staff, and Goldman executives are aiming to retain and hire more senior-level executives there.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
