\par\par \b \uc2 Starbucks Baristas Authorize Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Hamilton \par \uc2 227 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas on Wednesday authorized a strike after their union and the company failed to agree on a new contract.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United said 92% of its members voted to authorize the unfair labor practice strike, which comes just ahead of the company's promotional Red Cup Day on Nov. 13. If employees do strike, the actions could hit 25 or more cities, the union said.\par \par \uc2 "We are disappointed that Workers United, who only represents around 4% of our partners, has voted to authorize a strike instead of returning to the bargaining table," \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokeswoman Jaci Anderson said.\par \par \uc2 The union represents 9,500 employees in 550 shops, the Seattle-based coffee chain said.\par \par \uc2 Union delegates rejected the company's contract offer in April, citing a lack of wage increases and benefits in the first year of the contract.\par \par \uc2 The union said it wasn't currently engaged in negotiations with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , and that it is waiting for a new contract proposal from the company.\par \par \uc2 Baristas are looking for the company to increase hours to address understaffing concerns, raise take-home pay and resolve labor law violations for union busting, the union said.\par \par \uc2 "A fair union contract and the resolution of hundreds of unfair labor practice charges are essential to the company's turnaround," said Jasmine Leli, a barista and strike captain.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020251106elb60001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
